(window.LADDER_CARDS["12·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["12·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"12·phys.1.1","q":"On this page, what are a force, a weight and a lever, and how are mass and weight kept apart?","a":"a force is a push or a pull, in newtons; a weight is the Earth's pull on a thing, a force; a lever is a stiff bar turning on a pivot; mass is in kilograms, and weight is mass times about 9.8 N for each kilogram","w":["a force is a mass in motion; weight and mass are two names for one number in kilograms","a force is a distance from the pivot; weight is measured in metres; a lever is any bar","a weight is a unit of force; a lever is a balance point; mass is in newtons"],"why":"A 20 kg child has a weight of about 196 N. Mass says how much stuff, and weight how hard the Earth pulls it."},
 {"u":"12·phys.2.1","q":"What does the law of the lever say, and why is balancing in kilograms safe?","a":"two weights balance at distances from the pivot in the reverse ratio of the weights, W₁/W₂ = d₂/d₁; the 9.8 that turns kilograms to newtons stands on both sides and cancels","w":["two weights balance at equal distances whatever they weigh; kilograms are forces","two weights balance when W₁ + d₁ = W₂ + d₂; the 9.8 adds to 0","the heavier weight sits farther out; kilograms and newtons are equal on Earth"],"why":"Three times the weight sits at a third of the distance. Multiplying both sides by the same number keeps the answers."},
 {"u":"12·phys.3.1","q":"What is the turning effect of a weight W at x about a pivot at p, and when does a bar with many weights balance?","a":"W · (x − p), signed by the side of the pivot; the bar balances at p exactly when the net turning effect T(p), the sum over the weights, is 0","w":["W + (x − p); when every weight is at the pivot","W · x; when the weights on the two sides are equal","W/(x − p); when T(p) is greatest"],"why":"The signed distance makes the two sides turn opposite ways. Balance is the two turnings cancelling, not the weights being equal."},
 {"u":"12·phys.4.1","q":"What does balance-point say, and what did its proof not use?","a":"a loaded bar, with weight out along it, has a place p strictly between its ends where T(p) = 0; the proof did not use the particular weights, how many there are, or that they hang at single places","w":["every bar balances at its middle; the proof did not use continuity","a bar balances only if its weights are equal; the proof did not use the pivot","the balance point is at the heaviest weight; the proof did not use in-between-value"],"why":"T is positive with the pivot at one end, negative at the other, and continuous in p. In-between-value does the rest, so a sack of sand or an oar balances too."},
 {"u":"12·phys.5.1","q":"How does halving find the balance point, and what does the balance-formula say?","a":"test the middle of a trap for T, see which way the bar turns, and keep the half where the sign still changes; the balance point is the weighted average of the positions, Σ Wᵢxᵢ / Σ Wᵢ","w":["weigh each half and keep the heavier; the balance point is the plain average of the positions","move the weights until the bar is level; the balance point is the middle of the bar","halve every weight; the balance point is the largest position"],"why":"The halving needs only the sign of T, never the weights or the formula. The formula comes from solving the linear T(p) = 0."},
 {"u":"12·phys.6.1","q":"How is a steelyard the law of the lever run backwards?","a":"the law is solved for the unknown weight: with the hook 5 cm from the pivot and a 1 kg poise at d cm, m · 5 = 1 · d, so the load is read off the poise's place","w":["the load is found by adding the poise to the bar's length","the poise is moved until it weighs as much as the load","the pivot is moved until the load is at the middle"],"why":"The distance d is directly proportional to the load, so the marks on the arm are evenly spaced."},
 {"u":"12·phys.7.1","q":"What is the mechanical advantage of a lever, and why is the forearm's less than 1?","a":"the ratio load/effort, which in balance equals the effort's distance from the pivot over the load's; the biceps pulls about 4 cm from the elbow and the hand is about 35 cm out, so it is about 4/35","w":["the effort times the load; the forearm is short","the load's distance over the effort's; the elbow is not a pivot","the weight of the lever; muscle is lighter than bone"],"why":"The muscle must pull about nine times the load. The body is buying distance, not force."},
 {"u":"12·phys.8.1","q":"What price does a lever charge for multiplying a force, and why does energy pass through unchanged?","a":"distance: each place moves in proportion to its distance from the pivot, so the small force moves farther by the same ratio; force times distance, the work, is the same on both sides","w":["time: the load rises later than the effort moves","weight: the lever must be heavier than the load","nothing: a long enough lever gives both more force and more distance"],"why":"A crowbar that lifts a stone 1 cm moves your hands 12 cm with a twelfth of the force. An ideal lever gives a big force or a long distance, not both."},
 {"u":"12·phys.9.1","q":"What does gear-train say, and which two moves are the ones to watch?","a":"a train of meshes multiplies their ratios, taking t turns to (m₁m₂⋯m_k)t; do not add the ratios, and do not count an idler, whose teeth cancel","w":["the ratios add along the train; do not multiply them, and count each idler twice","the last gear alone sets the ratio; watch the first gear and the shaft","every mesh halves the turning; watch for odd numbers of teeth"],"why":"The second mesh acts on what the first produced, so scales compose by multiplying. An idler changes only which way the output turns."},
 {"u":"12·phys.10.1","q":"Why is “the balance point is the plain average of the positions” a move to watch out for?","a":"each position counts as heavily as its weight: the balance point is the weighted average, and it equals the plain average only when the weights are equal","w":["the balance point is always at the heaviest weight","positions cannot be averaged, only weights","the plain average is right whenever the bar is stiff"],"why":"Move the 5 kg weight and the plain average shifts less than the balance point does. A heavy weight pulls the balance toward itself."},
 {"u":"12·phys.11.1","q":"Handed only the directions a bar turned at the places you tested, what can you read off?","a":"the trap the balance point is in: it lies between two tested places where the bar turned opposite ways","w":["the weights on the bar","the exact balance point","nothing, without the formula"],"why":"T changes sign across the balance point and is continuous. Opposite turnings at two pivots trap it between them."});
/* end phase F und_r12 */
/* phase F und_r13: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13·phys.1.1","q":"What are the velocity and the acceleration at an instant, and what does each sign say?","a":"the velocity is the slope of the position, and the acceleration is the slope of the velocity; the velocity's sign says which way along the track the body moves, and the acceleration's says which way the velocity is changing","w":["the velocity is the position over the time, and the acceleration is the velocity over the time; the signs say how heavy the body is","the velocity is the chord slope over the whole motion, and the acceleration is its square; the signs are always positive","the velocity is the speed, and the acceleration is the speed's size; neither has a sign"],"why":"v(t) = x′(t), the limit of the average velocities. The speed is |v(t)|, velocity without its sign."},
 {"u":"13·phys.2.1","q":"What do the slopes of x = ½kt² give, and which flag of 9·phys does that close?","a":"the velocity kt and the acceleration k; 9·phys had to assume what speed at an instant meant, and the slope now defines it and agrees","w":["the velocity ½kt and the acceleration ½k; 9·phys had assumed the body had mass","the velocity k and the acceleration 0; 9·phys had assumed the track was level","the velocity kt² and the acceleration 2kt; 9·phys had assumed no air"],"why":"Power for slopes and constant-factor for slopes give ½k · 2t = kt. A constant second slope is what uniformly accelerated means."},
 {"u":"13·phys.3.1","q":"For a ball thrown up at 19.6 m/s, what are the velocity and the acceleration at the top?","a":"the velocity is 0 and the acceleration is −9.8 m/s²","w":["both are 0","the velocity is 19.6 m/s and the acceleration is 0","the velocity is −9.8 m/s and the acceleration is 0"],"why":"19.6 − 9.8t = 0 at t = 2 s, at height 19.6 m. Zero velocity and zero acceleration are different things: one is a value of the velocity, the other its slope."},
 {"u":"13·phys.4.1","q":"How do average velocity and velocity at an instant differ, and when do they meet?","a":"the average over a stretch is a chord slope and the velocity at an instant is a slope; for the falling motion they meet at the halfway instant, and for other motions they need not","w":["they are the same number for every motion","the average is always the larger; they meet only at rest","the average is the plain average of the end velocities; they meet at the end"],"why":"A dropped stone averages 14.7 m/s over 3 s and has 29.4 m/s at 3 s. For x = t³ over [0, 2] the chord slope is 4, the halfway velocity 3, the end average 6."},
 {"u":"13·phys.5.1","q":"What does the second law of motion say, and why does a heavy body not fall faster?","a":"a force F acting alone on a mass m gives it at every instant the acceleration F/m; a falling body's weight is m · g, so its acceleration is (m · g)/m = g, whatever its mass","w":["a force gives a body the velocity F/m; a heavy body does fall faster","a force F gives the acceleration F · m; a heavy body is pulled less","a force sets the position; all bodies have the same weight"],"why":"Force sets the second slope. The units check: kg · m/s² is the newton. The mass cancels when the air is ignored."},
 {"u":"13·phys.6.1","q":"What does the air do to a falling body, read from slopes alone?","a":"it slows the body more the faster it goes, so the acceleration shrinks toward 0 and the velocity levels off at a terminal velocity; the position graph straightens","w":["it makes the acceleration grow without end","it brings the velocity back to 0","it changes nothing: the acceleration stays g"],"why":"On the velocity picture the graph levels off, and its slope, the acceleration, heads to 0. A skydiver lying flat levels off at about 50 to 55 m/s."},
 {"u":"13·phys.7.1","q":"How do you run a velocity back to a position, and what can the velocity not see?","a":"find a function whose slope is the velocity, then fix the start with one more fact; the velocity cannot see where the body started, since a constant has slope 0","w":["multiply the velocity by the time; the velocity cannot see the acceleration","take the slope of the velocity; the velocity cannot see the time","divide the velocity by the time; the velocity cannot see the speed"],"why":"v = 9.8t + 2 runs back to 4.9t² + 2t + x₀, and x(0) = 0 picks one. Every position that fits differs from it by a constant."},
 {"u":"13·phys.8.1","q":"Why is “a = 0 at the top” a move to watch out for?","a":"at the top the velocity is 0, and its slope, the acceleration, is still −9.8 m/s²","w":["at the top the acceleration is 9.8 m/s² upward","at the top the velocity is not 0","the ball has no top"],"why":"A value of 0 says nothing of the slope there. The velocity passes through 0 while changing at the same rate as before."});
/* end phase F und_r13 */
/* phase F und_r14: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["14·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["14·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"14·phys.1.1","q":"What is momentum, and how does the second law read in terms of it?","a":"the mass times the velocity, with the velocity's sign; the total force on a body is the slope of its momentum","w":["the mass times the speed, never negative; the force is the momentum over the time","half the mass times the velocity squared; the force is the momentum","the mass times the acceleration; the force is the slope of the position"],"why":"For a constant mass (mv)′ = m · v′, by constant-factor for slopes. The form F = p′ keeps working where the mass changes, as for a rocket."},
 {"u":"14·phys.2.1","q":"What do the first and third laws say, and which is a law of nature here?","a":"a body with no force on it keeps its velocity; two bodies acting on each other push with forces equal in size and opposite in direction at every instant; the third is the law of nature, and the first reads as zero-slope-is-constant","w":["a body with no force stops; forces between two bodies add; both are techniques","a body keeps its position; the heavier body pushes harder; the first is the law of nature","a body speeds up without force; reactions come later than actions; neither is a law"],"why":"With F = p′, no force means p′ = 0 on an interval, so p is constant. Physicists keep the first law because it says in which frames the second is read."},
 {"u":"14·phys.3.1","q":"How is conservation of momentum proved, and what was not needed?","a":"the second law for each cart and the third law make the slope of the total momentum 0, so it is constant by zero-slope-is-constant; nothing about the contact was needed, not how long it lasted nor how the force rose and fell","w":["by measuring many collisions; the masses were not needed","by keeping the kinetic energy; the third law was not needed","by average-slope on each cart; the second law was not needed"],"why":"(p₁ + p₂)′ = F₂₁ + F₁₂ = 0. The law of nature is the third law; the conservation is zero-slope-is-constant."},
 {"u":"14·phys.4.1","q":"What happens to momentum and kinetic energy when railcars couple?","a":"the momentum is kept and some kinetic energy is not: it goes to heat, sound and bent metal","w":["both are kept","the kinetic energy is kept and the momentum is lost","both are lost"],"why":"30 000 kg at 2 m/s into 20 000 kg at rest leaves at 1.2 m/s. Kinetic energy falls from 60 000 J to 36 000 J."},
 {"u":"14·phys.5.1","q":"What do the two laws together say about a bounce, and what does the third line mean?","a":"momentum kept and kinetic energy kept fix both velocities after; the third line says the speed at which the carts approach equals the speed at which they separate","w":["they fix only the total speed; the third line says the carts stop","they leave the velocities free; the third line says the masses are equal","they say the carts stick; the third line says the heavier cart is faster"],"why":"With equal masses and the second at rest, the striker stops dead and the other leaves with its velocity. 1 kg at 3 m/s into 2 kg at rest gives −1 m/s and 2 m/s."},
 {"u":"14·phys.6.1","q":"What is impulse, and what does a helmet change?","a":"the change of a body's momentum over an interval; a helmet does not change the impulse, it stretches the time and so lowers the average force","w":["the force at one instant; a helmet lowers the impulse","the momentum itself; a helmet lowers the rider's mass","the average force; a helmet shortens the time"],"why":"A 70 kg rider stopped from 8 m/s takes 560 N · s either way: 28 000 N on average over 0.02 s, 2800 N over 0.2 s. By average-slope some instant has exactly the average force."},
 {"u":"14·phys.7.1","q":"What happens at the instant of greatest squeeze of a spring between two carts?","a":"the squeeze has slope 0 there by flat-top, so the two velocities are equal: both carts move together, at the velocity conservation gives, and the missing kinetic energy sits in the spring","w":["both carts are at rest and all the energy is lost","the carts move apart at their fastest","the first cart has its starting velocity and the second is at rest"],"why":"s′ = v₁ − v₂. For 1 kg at 2 m/s into 1 kg at rest, the common velocity is 1 m/s and 1 J of the 2 J is in the spring."},
 {"u":"14·phys.8.1","q":"Why is “a padded vest lowers the impulse” a move to watch out for?","a":"the change of momentum is the same; the vest lowers the average force by taking longer","w":["the vest raises the impulse","the vest changes the rider's momentum before the stop","impulse has nothing to do with force"],"why":"Impulse is the change of momentum, fixed by the stop. Padding stretches the time, and the average force is the impulse over the time."});
/* end phase F und_r14 */
/* phase F und_r15: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["15·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["15·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"15·phys.1.1","q":"What is a plate on this page, and what is the physics premise?","a":"a flat sheet of even thickness cut to the region under a continuous f ≥ 0; the premise is that a piece of plate weighs in proportion to its area","w":["a bar with weights hung on it; the premise is that every strip weighs the same","a solid block; the premise is that weight grows with height alone","a region with no weight; the premise is that area is a slope"],"why":"With ρ the weight of one square metre, a plate of area A weighs ρA. A thin upright strip weighs between ρmᵢΔxᵢ and ρMᵢΔxᵢ."},
 {"u":"15·phys.2.1","q":"What does balance-plate say for the balance across, and why does ρ drop out?","a":"the plate balances across at X = ∫ x f(x) dx divided by ∫ f(x) dx; ρ is a factor of the whole turning effect, so it does not move the place where that is 0","w":["the plate balances at the middle of [u, v]; ρ drops out because the plate is thin","the plate balances at ∫ x f(x) dx; ρ drops out because it is 1","the plate balances where f is greatest; ρ is not in the turning effect"],"why":"T(p) = ρ(∫ x f − p ∫ f), trapped strip by strip between lower and upper sums. A place is the turning effect shared by the weight."},
 {"u":"15·phys.3.1","q":"How does the balance up come out, and where does the lifting eye go on the plate under x² on [0, 1]?","a":"Y = ∫ ½f(x)² dx divided by ∫ f(x) dx, since each upright strip turns as if its weight sat at its middle; under x² on [0, 1] the balance point is (0.75, 0.3)","w":["Y is half the average height; the eye goes at (0.5, 1/6)","Y = ∫ f(x)² dx; the eye goes at (1, 1)","Y is half the greatest height; the eye goes at (0.5, 0.5)"],"why":"∫₀¹ ½x⁴ dx = 1/10 and ∫₀¹ x² dx = ⅓, so Y = 3/10. Half the average height, 1/6, is too low: the tall strips carry their weight high."},
 {"u":"15·phys.4.1","q":"What does the plate-pieces technique say, and how does it handle a hole?","a":"each piece turns the plate as if its whole weight sat at its own balance point, so the whole balances at the pieces' balance points weighted by their areas; a hole is a piece with its weight taken away","w":["the whole balances at the average of the pieces' balance points, whatever their areas; a hole is ignored","the heavier piece alone decides the balance; a hole is added in","pieces cannot be combined; a hole needs a new integral"],"why":"X = (A₁X₁ + A₂X₂)/(A₁ + A₂). Run backwards: (A − A_c) · Y_rest = A · Y − A_c · Y_c."},
 {"u":"15·phys.5.1","q":"Why is “∫ x · x² dx = ∫ x dx · ∫ x² dx” a move to watch out for?","a":"areas add and scale; they do not multiply: on [0, 1] the left is ¼ and the right is ½ · ⅓ = 1/6","w":["the left side is not integrable","the two sides are equal only on [0, 1]","the right side should be a sum"],"why":"The integral is linear, for sums and constant factors only. The balance across needs the integral of the product itself."});
/* end phase F und_r15 */
/* phase F und_r16: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16·phys.1.1","q":"How do displacement and speed come from their rates, and what does a constant push give?","a":"the displacement is the area under v and the change in velocity is the area under a; with a constant, x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²","w":["the displacement is the slope of v and the change in velocity is the slope of a; with a constant, x = x₀ + at","the displacement is the area under a and the change in velocity is the area under x; with a constant, x = v₀t","the displacement is v times a; with a constant, x = x₀ + v₀t + at²"],"why":"Both are area-is-a-change, since x′ = v and v′ = a. Integrating a gives v₀ + at, and integrating that gives the ½at²."},
 {"u":"16·phys.2.1","q":"What does the impulse technique say, and what does a 2 ms push of at most 9000 N do to a ball?","a":"the change in momentum is the area under the force, ∫F dt; the push gives 12 N·s, so a 0.15 kg ball gains 80 m/s, under an average force of 6000 N","w":["the change in momentum is the greatest force times the time; the push gives 18 N·s and the ball gains 120 m/s","the change in momentum is the force's slope; the ball gains 9000 m/s","the change in kinetic energy is ∫F dt; the push gives 12 J and the ball gains 12 m/s"],"why":"F = p′, so area-is-a-change applies with p the antiderivative. The force rises and falls, so its area is less than 9000 × 0.002."},
 {"u":"16·phys.3.1","q":"What does work-energy say, and how does substitution for areas prove it?","a":"the work ∫F dx done by the total force equals the change in ½mv²; putting x = x(t) turns it into ∫ma·v dt, and mav is the slope of ½mv²","w":["the work ∫F dx equals the change in mv; putting x = x(t) turns it into ∫F dt","the work F·d equals ½mv² for any force; no substitution is needed","the work ∫F dx equals the kinetic energy at the start; substitution removes the end"],"why":"With x = x(t), dx becomes v dt and F is ma. The chain technique gives (½mv²)′ = mv·a, so area-is-a-change closes it."},
 {"u":"16·phys.4.1","q":"What does a spring keep through a whole swing?","a":"the sum ½mv² + ½kx², the same at every instant","w":["the speed v, the same at every instant","the product of ½mv² and ½kx²","the kinetic energy ½mv² alone"],"why":"The spring's work from x₁ to x₂ is ½kx₁² − ½kx₂², and work-energy sets it equal to the change in ½mv². What one term loses the other gains."},
 {"u":"16·phys.5.1","q":"Why does escaping the Earth take a finite amount of work, and what speed pays it?","a":"the work from R to b is GMm(1/R − 1/b), which heads to GMm/R as b grows without end; the speed √(2GM/R), about 11.2 km/s","w":["the pull stops at a certain height, so the work stops too; about 11.2 m/s","the work grows without end, so no speed pays it","the work is GMm/R² times the distance gone; about 7.9 km/s"],"why":"Out-to-forever gives the limit, since 1/b → 0. Then ½mv² ≥ GMm/R, and the mass m cancels."},
 {"u":"16·phys.6.1","q":"What is the electric potential energy of two charges, and what answers 13·eng's question?","a":"U(r) = kq₁q₂/r, the work the push does as the charges fly apart to forever; power is the rate of work, P = W′, which is Fv by the chain technique","w":["U(r) = kq₁q₂/r², the push itself; power is the work, P = W","U(r) = kq₁q₂ r, growing with distance; power is F/v","U(r) = ½kq₁q₂, the same at every distance; power is the total work over a day"],"why":"∫ᵣ^∞ kq₁q₂/x² dx = kq₁q₂/r by out-to-forever. With W′(x) = F and x′ = v, the chain gives W′(t) = Fv."},
 {"u":"16·phys.7.1","q":"Why is “∫ F dt = Δ(½mv²)” a move to watch out for?","a":"force over time gives the change in momentum, Δ(mv); it is force over distance, ∫F dx, that gives Δ(½mv²)","w":["force over time gives the work, not the energy","∫F dt is always zero over a whole swing","the ½ should be left out: ∫F dt = Δ(mv²)"],"why":"Impulse integrates against t, work against x. The letter after d says which change the area is."});
/* end phase F und_r16 */
/* phase F und_r17: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["17·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["17·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"17·phys.1.1","q":"What are a wave's length and frequency, and how do they relate?","a":"the length λ is the distance from one crest to the next, and the frequency f is the number of crests passing a point each second; fλ = c, the wave's speed","w":["λ is the height of a crest and f its width; f + λ = c","λ is the time between crests and f the distance; f = λc","λ and f are both counts of crests; f/λ = c"],"why":"For light c = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s. Violet is near 400 nm and red near 700 nm."},
 {"u":"17·phys.2.1","q":"What does endless-glow say, and where is the fault?","a":"with the classical formula u(λ) = C kT/λ⁴, the area ∫₀^λ₁ u dλ diverges, so the total energy is not a number; the fault is at the short waves, the violet end and past it","w":["the area converges to kT; the fault is at the long waves","the area is 0 at every temperature; the fault is in the count C","the area diverges because of the red end; short waves carry nothing"],"why":"∫ from δ of λ⁻⁴ is ⅓(δ⁻³ − λ₁⁻³), which passes every bound as δ → 0⁺. Measured glows rise to a peak and fall to 0 at short waves."},
 {"u":"17·phys.3.1","q":"What does lumpy-average say, and what does it do to the glow formula?","a":"a wave that may hold any energy averages kT, and one that may hold only whole lumps nhf averages hf/(e^(hf/kT) − 1), less than kT and heading to kT as hf/kT → 0; put in place of kT, it starves the short waves and gives the lump law of glow","w":["both averages are kT; the lumps change nothing in the formula","the lumpy average is hf, more than kT; it makes the short waves brighter","the lumpy average is kT/hf; it removes the long waves"],"why":"With q = e^(−hf/kT), Σ nqⁿ / Σ qⁿ = q/(1 − q). Long waves behave classically; a lump much larger than kT is almost never given out."},
 {"u":"17·phys.4.1","q":"What do finite-glow and fourth-power say?","a":"I = ∫₀^∞ x³/(eˣ − 1) dx converges; and the lump law's total energy is C(kT)⁴I/(hc)³, a constant times T⁴, so doubling the temperature makes the glow sixteen times as great","w":["I diverges; and the total energy is a constant times T","I converges; and the total energy is a constant times T², so doubling T gives four times the glow","I = 0; and the total energy does not depend on T"],"why":"Near 0 the integrand is at most x², and past 1 at most 6250/x². The substitution x = hc/(λkT) takes out the powers of T."},
 {"u":"17·phys.5.1","q":"What does peak-moves say, and how hot is the Sun's surface?","a":"u(λ) is greatest where λT = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m·K, so a hotter body peaks at a shorter wave; sunlight peaks near 502 nm, so the surface is near 5770 K","w":["the peak wavelength rises in step with T; about 502 K","λ/T is fixed; about 288 K","the peak is at the same wavelength for every T; the Sun cannot be read this way"],"why":"The peak is at x* ≈ 4.965, the one positive solution of 5(1 − e⁻ˣ) = x, found by in-between-value and halving. The Earth, near 288 K, peaks at about 10 μm."},
 {"u":"17·phys.6.1","q":"What does the photoelectric technique say, and which three facts does it explain?","a":"each photon gives its whole energy hf to one electron, which needs at least φ to leave, so K_max = hf − φ and none is freed when f < φ/h; so there is a least frequency, brighter light frees more electrons but not faster ones, and they come out at once","w":["the energy of a freed electron grows with brightness; so dim light frees slow electrons after a wait","K_max = φ − hf; so only red light frees electrons","each electron collects many photons; so brighter light frees faster electrons"],"why":"One photon to one electron: brightness counts electrons, and frequency sets their energy."},
 {"u":"17·phys.7.1","q":"What do bare-planet and greenhouse-layer say, and what fills the 33 K gap?","a":"a planet with no air is in balance at Tₑ = (S(1 − α)/(4σ))^(1/4), about 255 K for the Earth; with a layer absorbing a fraction ε of the ground's infrared, Tₛ = Tₑ(2/(2 − ε))^(1/4); the air fills the gap to the measured 288 K","w":["a bare Earth would be at 288 K; a layer cools the ground by 33 K","Tₑ = S(1 − α)/4; each gas adds a fixed number of kelvins","Tₛ = Tₑ(1 + ε); the gap is filled by heat from inside the Earth"],"why":"Out equals in: σTₑ⁴ = S(1 − α)/4. The layer sends half of what it absorbs back down, and enters under a fourth root."},
 {"u":"17·phys.8.1","q":"Why is \"brighter red light frees faster electrons\" a move to watch out for?","a":"brightness counts photons, and each red photon still carries hf < φ; so red light frees nothing, however bright","w":["red photons carry the most energy, so the electrons are already as fast as can be","brightness lowers the frequency","brighter light frees slower electrons"],"why":"For the metal of the worked lines, φ ≈ 2.29 eV and a photon at 700 nm carries 1.77 eV."});
/* end phase F und_r17 */
/* phase F und_r18: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["18·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["18·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"18·phys.1.1","q":"What do the seven places of Mars show about its path?","a":"the distance from the sun, r = √(x² + y²), runs from 1.381 to 1.665 AU; so the path is not a round one about the sun, on which r would never change","w":["r is the same at all seven places, so the path is a circle about the sun","r doubles from nearest to farthest, so the path is a parabola","the seven places lie on one line, so Mars moves straight"],"why":"Distance-laws on a table of places worked out from modern tables, with the sun at (0, 0)."},
 {"u":"18·phys.2.1","q":"What does the lift technique say?","a":"raise each place to the height of its distance r, onto the upper cone; if r = ℓ − ex for every place, the raised points lie on one plane, and the path is a cut of the cone: an ellipse when 0 ≤ e < 1, a parabola when e = 1, a hyperbola when e > 1","w":["if r is the same at every place, the path is an ellipse","if r = ℓ − ex, the path is a circle of radius ℓ","raise each place by x; if the raised points lie on a sphere, the path is an ellipse"],"why":"Put the plane's z = ℓ − ex into x² + y² = z², then cone-cut (18·cb16). So the test is a line: draw r against x."},
 {"u":"18·phys.3.1","q":"How is the line of r against x read, and what does it give for Mars?","a":"two places fix the line, by two-point and point-slope, and the other five test it: slope ≈ −0.0935 and ℓ ≈ 1.510, with every other place within a thousandth or two; so e ≈ 0.0935, and the path is a nearly round ellipse","w":["all seven places are averaged; the slope is 0, so the path is a circle","two places fix the line and the rest are ignored; e ≈ 1.510, a hyperbola","the line is drawn by eye; it gives e ≈ 0.967, a long thin ellipse"],"why":"The lifted places lie on one plane, tilted much less than the cone. The misses are about the rounding of the table."},
 {"u":"18·phys.4.1","q":"Where does the sun sit on the orbit, and why not at the centre?","a":"at a focus, with the other focus empty; on an ellipse centred at the sun, r would be the same at x and at −x, and the table says no: 1.407 near x = 1.114 against 1.624 near x = −1.216","w":["at the centre; the table's two sides agree","at the far end of the long axis; r is greatest there","at neither focus nor centre; the table cannot say where"],"why":"Completing the square puts the centre about 0.142 AU from the sun, and the two distances to the foci add to about 3.047 at every place."},
 {"u":"18·phys.5.1","q":"How do the nearest and farthest distances give the whole line, and what do the three cuts look like in the sky?","a":"with q nearest and Q farthest, ℓ = 2qQ/(q + Q) and e = (Q − q)/(Q + q); Mars's e ≈ 0.09 is a nearly round ellipse, a returning comet's e ≈ 0.967 a long ellipse with a plane nearly as steep as the cone, and a body from outside with e ≈ 1.20 passes once on a branch of a hyperbola","w":["ℓ = q + Q and e = Q/q; all three bodies move on circles","ℓ = (q + Q)/2 and e = Q − q; the comet moves on a parabola and never returns","the two distances give only α; the three cuts are never seen in the sky"],"why":"Orbit-ends (18h) run backwards. That a planet moves on an ellipse with the sun at a focus is a measured law, judged against the error of the measurements."},
 {"u":"18·phys.6.1","q":"Why is \"r changes, so the path is not round, so it is an ellipse\" a move to watch out for?","a":"a path that is not round need not be an ellipse: that follows only if r = ℓ − ex, so that the raised points lie on one plane","w":["a path on which r changes is always a hyperbola","r does not change on Mars's path","every closed path is an ellipse"],"why":"Lift technique: the test is whether r against x is a straight line."});
/* end phase F und_r18 */
/* phase F und_r20: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["20·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["20·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"20·phys.1.1","q":"What does the flip-the-target technique say, and why is the flipped point B′ brought in?","a":"the shortest path from A to the mirror to B touches the mirror where the segment AB′ crosses it; every point P of the mirror is as far from B′ as from B, so the bent path is as long as a path from A to B′, and that is shortest when straight","w":["the shortest path touches the mirror at the point nearest A; B′ marks where the light ends","the shortest path touches the mirror half way between A and B; B′ is the midpoint","the shortest path touches the mirror where AB′ crosses it; B′ is brought in because the flip doubles every distance"],"why":"d(A, P) + d(P, B′) ≥ d(A, B′), with equality exactly when P is on the segment (18b)."},
 {"u":"20·phys.2.1","q":"What does the corner technique say, and which technique of 20e proves it?","a":"two mirrors at right angles send every ray back the way it came, whatever its direction; two flips across lines that cross make a turn, here the half turn (a, b) ↦ (−a, −b)","w":["two mirrors at right angles send every ray off at a quarter turn; by parallel-flips","two mirrors at right angles send back only the rays aimed at the corner's middle; by crossing-flips","two parallel mirrors send every ray back the way it came; by parallel-flips"],"why":"(a, b) ↦ (a, −b) ↦ (−a, −b)."},
 {"u":"20·phys.3.1","q":"What does the bend technique say, and when do the quickest and the shortest path agree?","a":"the quickest path bends so that the run over the length, divided by the speed, is the same on both sides; they agree when the speeds are equal, and the path is straight","w":["the quickest path is always straight; they always agree","the quickest path bends so that the angles are in the ratio of the speeds; they agree when the mirror is flat","the quickest path bends so that the two lengths are equal; they agree when A and B are equally far from the surface"],"why":"T′(x) = x/(v₁ℓ₁) − (d − x)/(v₂ℓ₂), and its one zero is the least time."},
 {"u":"20·phys.4.1","q":"Why does light inside water past a certain slant have no path out?","a":"the run over the length in air must be 1.33 times that in water, and a run is never longer than its length; past 0.75 in water there is no path out, and the light bounces back","w":["the water slows the light until it stops","past that slant the light runs faster than it can in air","the run over the length in air is 0.75 times that in water, so every ray gets out"],"why":"1.33 r ≤ 1 needs r ≤ 0.75; a glass thread carries light round a bend this way."},
 {"u":"20·phys.5.1","q":"Why is \"the bounce is at the point of the mirror nearest A\" a move to watch out for?","a":"the bounce is where the segment AB′ crosses the mirror; the nearest point to A shortens the first leg and not the whole path","w":["the bounce is at the point of the mirror nearest B","the bounce is half way between the points nearest A and nearest B, always","the bounce is at the point nearest A only when the mirror is curved"],"why":"The whole path is as long as a path from A to B′, and that is shortest when straight."});
/* end phase F und_r20 */
/* phase F und_r21: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21·phys.1.1","q":"What does the vector technique say, and what are the parts of a force of size F at angle θ from east?","a":"to add forces, add their parts across and their parts up, and the size of the sum comes from the cosine-side technique; the parts are F cos θ east and F sin θ north","w":["to add forces, add their sizes; the parts are F sin θ east and F cos θ north","to add forces, add their angles and multiply their sizes; the parts are F cos θ and F sin θ","to add forces, add their parts; the size of the sum is the sum of the sizes"],"why":"300 N at 0° and 400 N at 60° give (500, 346.4), of size about 608.3 N."},
 {"u":"21·phys.2.1","q":"What does the slope technique say, and why does the block's weight drop out?","a":"a block holds on a slope exactly when tan α ≤ μ, whatever it weighs; both the pull down the slope, W sin α, and the most friction can hold, μ W cos α, carry W, so it divides out","w":["a block holds exactly when sin α ≤ μ; the weight drops out because friction ignores it","a block holds exactly when tan α ≤ μ W; heavier blocks hold on steeper slopes","a block holds exactly when tan α ≥ μ; the weight drops out because it presses straight down"],"why":"W sin α ≤ μ W cos α, divided by W cos α."},
 {"u":"21·phys.3.1","q":"What does the round technique say, and which way does p″ point?","a":"going round a circle at speed v takes a pull of v²/r per unit of mass; p″ = −ω² p points toward the centre","w":["going round at a steady speed takes no pull; p″ = 0","it takes a pull of v²/r; p″ points outward, away from the centre","it takes a pull of v/r; p″ points along the motion"],"why":"|p″| = rω² = v²/r, with v = rω."},
 {"u":"21·phys.4.1","q":"What does the tilt technique say, and what do the two parts of the road's push N do?","a":"a curve tilted so that tan α = v²/(rg) holds a car at speed v with no friction at all; N cos α, up, holds the weight, and N sin α, toward the centre, gives the pull","w":["a curve tilted so that tan α = rg/v² holds the car; N sin α holds the weight","a curve tilted so that sin α = v²/(rg) holds a car at every speed; N cos α gives the pull","a tilted curve needs more friction than a flat one; both parts of N point up"],"why":"Divide N sin α = mv²/r by N cos α = mg."},
 {"u":"21·phys.5.1","q":"Why is \"going round at a steady speed needs no force\" a move to watch out for?","a":"the speed is steady but the direction changes, and that takes a pull of v²/r toward the centre","w":["a steady speed needs a force along the motion to keep it up","it needs a force outward, to balance the turn","it needs no force as long as the circle is large"],"why":"p″ = −ω² p is not 0."},
 {"u":"21·phys.6.1","q":"Why do the across and up parts of a thrown stone run without touching each other, what does the slant-throw technique say, and why do 30° and 60° land a stone at the same place?","a":"weight pulls only down, so nothing acts on the across part; a stone thrown at speed v and angle θ lands v² sin 2θ/g away, farthest at 45°; sin 60° = sin 120°, so two angles that add to 90° land it at the same place","w":["the two parts run apart because the air drags on one only; the stone lands v² sin θ/g away, farthest at 90°","weight pulls only down; the stone lands v² sin 2θ/g away, farthest at 45°; 30° and 60° land together because both throws stay up as long","weight pulls on both parts; the stone lands v² cos 2θ/g away, farthest at 0°"],"why":"The time aloft is 2v sin θ/g, and 2 sin θ cos θ = sin 2θ."});
/* end phase F und_r21 */
/* phase F und_r22: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["22·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["22·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"22·phys.1.1","q":"Each bounce reaches a share r of the last height. How far does a ball dropped from h travel in all, and which technique sums the bounces?","a":"h(1 + r)/(1 − r): h down, then each bounce up and back, h + 2(rh + r²h + ⋯), summed by endless-ratio-sum (15i) with first term rh and ratio r","w":["h/(1 − r): each bounce counted once, by endless-ratio-sum","endlessly far, since the bounces never end","h(1 + r)/(1 − r), summed by the grouping technique"],"why":"From 30 cm with r = 23/30 that is about 2.27 m."},
 {"u":"22·phys.2.1","q":"Within what time do endlessly many bounces end, and why do the hop times shrink by √r, not r?","a":"t₀(1 + √r)/(1 − √r) seconds, with t₀ the first fall; a fall from height H takes √(2H/g), so the time goes as the root of the height","w":["t₀(1 + r)/(1 − r) seconds; the hop times shrink by r, as the heights do","they never end; endlessly many hops take endless time","t₀/(1 − √r) seconds; each hop is one fall, not up and down"],"why":"The ratio √r is still below 1, so the times make an endless ratio sum that settles."},
 {"u":"22·phys.3.1","q":"What can the ear measure from two neighbouring gaps between clicks?","a":"the share: neighbouring gaps stand in the ratio √r, so r is the square of that ratio, and the time left after a click is the next gap over 1 − √r","w":["the share: neighbouring gaps stand in the ratio r","the drop height: the gaps are all equal to 2t₀","nothing: the gaps shrink too fast to time"],"why":"Gaps of 0.400 s and 0.350 s give √r = 0.875 and r ≈ 0.766."},
 {"u":"22·phys.4.1","q":"Each real bounce presses the ball to the table for at least a fixed time. What follows, and what does it show about the share law?","a":"bounces that each take at least a fixed time cannot be endlessly many in a few seconds; so the share law must fail at some small height: it was measured over a range and has been read past it","w":["the ball bounces endlessly often, but only for a few seconds","the share law holds at every height, and the contact time shrinks with the hops","the time technique is false at every height"],"why":"n bounces take at least nτ, which passes every bound, yet the ball rests in seconds."},
 {"u":"22·phys.5.1","q":"What is out of place in “the hops shrink, so they end in a finite time”?","a":"shrinking is not enough (22d): hops of 1, ½, ⅓, … seconds pass every bound; these end because they shrink by the fixed ratio √r < 1","w":["the hops do not shrink","hops that shrink never end","nothing: times that head to 0 always add to a finite time"],"why":"The harmonic sum is the standing warning."});
/* end phase F und_r22 */
/* phase F und_r23: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23·phys.1.1","q":"What is n for a clear stuff, and what does light keep when it crosses from one into another?","a":"n is how many times slower light runs in it than in empty space; crossing, light keeps n sin θ the same on both sides, with θ the slant from the upright to the surface; it comes from 20·phys's least time, the run over the length being sin θ","w":["n is the speed of light in it; light keeps θ the same on both sides","n is how many times slower light runs in it; light keeps n·θ the same, by least distance","n is its thickness; light keeps sin θ/n the same on both sides"],"why":"The run along the surface over the length of the path, divided by the speed, is the same on both sides."},
 {"u":"23·phys.2.1","q":"Where does a ray entering a round glass surface at height y cross the axis, and why has a round surface no exact focus?","a":"R/(√(n² − s²) − √(1 − s²)) past the centre, with s = y/R; the crossing depends on s, so each height has its own crossing","w":["nR/(n − 1) past the centre, at every height; the focus is exact","R/(n − 1) past the centre; there is no focus because the glass is thick","at the centre; a round surface sends every ray through its centre"],"why":"The formula is exact, and untidy."},
 {"u":"23·phys.3.1","q":"Where do rays near the axis gather behind a round glass front, by how much does a ray at height y fall short, and what is a focus on that page?","a":"at nR/(n − 1); a ray at height y falls short of that spot by about y²/(2n(n − 1)R); a focus is a short stretch of the axis, and you choose its length when you choose the opening","w":["at R/(n − 1); every ray meets there exactly; a focus is a point","at nR/(n − 1); a ray at height y overshoots by y/n; a focus is the centre of the ball","at nR; rays fall short by y²; a focus is the whole axis"],"why":"The binomial series with power ½ hugs each square root for small s."},
 {"u":"23·phys.4.1","q":"What is a star's parallax, how far is the star, and where does the doubt in the distance come from?","a":"p is half the full yearly swing: the slant at which one AU is seen from the star; the star is 1 AU/p away, too far by a share less than p²/2; every bit of doubt comes from measuring p","w":["p is the full yearly swing; the star is 2 AU/p away; the doubt comes from the small-slant reading","p is half the yearly swing; the star is p AU away; the doubt comes from the earth's speed","p is the slant of the star above the horizon; the star is 1 AU·p away; there is no doubt"],"why":"d = 1 AU/tan p, and tan p is p to within a share of p²: for Proxima Centauri under 7 × 10⁻¹²."},
 {"u":"23·phys.5.1","q":"What is out of place in “θ₁/θ₂ = n₂/n₁”, and in “a star's full yearly swing is its parallax”?","a":"the sines keep the ratio; the slants do only when they are small; and the swing is 2p: p is the slant of one AU","w":["the ratio is n₁/n₂; and the parallax is twice the swing","nothing in the first; in the second the swing is p/2","the slants are always equal; and the parallax is the swing in a day"],"why":"A small-slant reading is a reading with a known error, not an identity."});
/* end phase F und_r23 */
/* phase F und_r24: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["24·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24·phys.1.1","q":"How long does a mass on a spring take to swing, and which of C, φ and n come from the start?","a":"2π√(m/κ), however wide its swing and however it was started; C, the width, and φ, where in its swing it was at t = 0, come from the start; n = √(κ/m) comes from the spring and the mass","w":["2π√(κ/m), longer for a wider swing; all three come from the start","2π√(m/κ); n comes from the start, C and φ from the spring","2πm/κ; none comes from the start"],"why":"mx″ = −κx is y″ = −n²y."},
 {"u":"24·phys.2.1","q":"What fixes the width and top speed of a swinging spring, and why is the energy constant?","a":"the energy E = ½mx′² + ½κx²: C = √(2E/κ) and top speed nC; E′ = x′(mx″ + κx) = 0 by the rule, and zero slope means constant","w":["the mass alone; the energy falls each swing","the energy; it is constant because x′ = 0 at the ends","the period; the energy is constant because the spring is stiff"],"why":"A real spring loses a little each swing: the swing shrinks and the period does not."},
 {"u":"24·phys.3.1","q":"How long is a small swing of a pendulum there and back, and where does the approximation enter?","a":"2π√(L/g), whatever the weight; the rule is Lθ″ = −g sin θ, and sin θ is read as θ for small θ","w":["2π√(g/L); the approximation is in g","2π√(L/g); nothing is approximate","2π√(mL/g); the approximation is in the weight"],"why":"With sin θ ≈ θ it is the even-time rule with n² = g/L."},
 {"u":"24·phys.4.1","q":"How much longer does a pendulum released at A radians take, and what does that make of the even-time law?","a":"longer than the small-swing time by about a share A²/16; the law is kept with its exception rate known, and the case kept where that is small: clockmakers kept the swing small and the same from day to day","w":["shorter by A²/16; the law is given up","longer by a share A/2; the law holds only for springs","exactly the small-swing time; the law has no exception"],"why":"A swing that stays at 4° runs slow by a fixed share, set right once."},
 {"u":"24·phys.5.1","q":"What speed does a falling body with drag v′ = g − kv approach, and why does that state hold?","a":"g/k, where the drag matches the weight; the slope of g − kv in v is −k < 0, so the state left alone holds (24k)","w":["it gains speed without limit; no state holds","g·k; it holds because k > 0","g/k; it holds because the body reaches it in time 1/k"],"why":"v = (g/k)(1 − e^(−kt)) from rest."},
 {"u":"24·phys.6.1","q":"What is out of place in “v′ = g − kv, so the fall reaches g/k and stays”?","a":"it heads to g/k and never reaches it: the gap shrinks by a fixed factor in each stretch of time and is never 0","w":["it passes g/k and swings back","it reaches g/k at t = 1/k","nothing: a state that holds is reached"],"why":"g/k is a resting line, and no solution starting off it touches it."});
/* end phase F und_r24 */
/* phase F und_r26: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["26·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["26·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"26·phys.1.1","q":"What does the steady-stand-in technique say, and why is the stand-in V₀/√2?","a":"a voltage swinging between +V₀ and −V₀ warms a resistor as a steady V₀/√2 would, so a 120 V outlet swings to about 170 V; the warmth goes as the square of the voltage, and the square of a sine averages 1/2 over a turn","w":["it warms as a steady V₀/2 would; the voltage averages half its peak","it warms as a steady V₀ would; the peaks do all the warming","it warms as a steady 0 V would; a sine averages 0 over a turn"],"why":"Half the square, then the root: V₀/√2."},
 {"u":"26·phys.2.1","q":"What is the impedance Z of a resistor, a coil and a store, and what do its size and its turn mean?","a":"R, iωL and −i/(ωC), with V = ZI at pace ω; the size of Z is the ratio of the voltage swing to the current swing, and its turn is how far the current runs behind the voltage","w":["R, ωL and 1/(ωC), all real; the size is the warmth and there is no turn","R, iωL and −i/(ωC); the size is the pace and the turn is the voltage","R, i/(ωL) and −iωC; the size is the ratio of the swings and the turn is how far the current runs ahead"],"why":"A coil’s current runs a quarter turn behind; a store’s a quarter turn ahead."},
 {"u":"26·phys.3.1","q":"What does the row technique say, and why do a coil’s 37.7 Ω and a store’s 26.5 Ω not add to 64 Ω?","a":"in a row, impedances add as complex numbers and the current is the voltage over the total; the coil’s points straight up and the store’s straight down, so they mostly cancel, leaving 11.2 Ω upright","w":["in a row the sizes of the impedances add; 64 Ω is right","in a row impedances multiply; the two give about 1000 Ω","in a row only the resistance counts; the coil and the store add nothing at any pace"],"why":"Add first, then take the size."},
 {"u":"26·phys.4.1","q":"What does the resonance technique say, and why is the current largest at that pace?","a":"a coil and a store in a row pass the pace 1/√(LC) best; there iωL and −i/(ωC) cancel exactly, so only the resistance is in the way and |Z| is smallest","w":["they pass the pace √(LC) best; there the coil and the store each have their largest size","they pass the pace 1/(LC) best; there the resistance drops to 0","they pass every pace alike; the current is largest where the voltage is largest"],"why":"Turning C on the dial moves that pace: C picks the station."},
 {"u":"26·phys.5.1","q":"What does the sharpness technique say, and why must a tuned circuit hear a band and not a single pace?","a":"it passes at least half the power of every pace within ω₀/(2Q) of ω₀, with Q = ω₀L/R: the smaller the resistance, the narrower the band; a station’s sound lives in the paces just beside its own, so too narrow a band cuts its high notes","w":["it passes only the pace ω₀, and the larger Q the better the sound","it passes every pace within Q of ω₀; a wide band is always best, since no station is lost","it passes half the power at ω₀ itself; the band is fixed by C alone"],"why":"Choose the width, and say it."},
 {"u":"26·phys.6.1","q":"What does the pushed-swing technique say, and which number is the circuit’s Q?","a":"a damped spring pushed at its own pace swings ω₀/(2b) times as far as a steady push of the same size would hold it; the circuit’s Q is that same number, with 2b = R/L","w":["it swings 2b/ω₀ times as far; Q is R/L","it swings without ceiling at its own pace; Q is the number of swings before rest","it swings ω₀ times as far; Q is 1/√(LC)"],"why":"With b = 0.05 a playground swing builds twenty times the held push."},
 {"u":"26·phys.7.1","q":"What is wrong with “|Z| = R + ωL + 1/(ωC)”?","a":"the parts point different ways, R along the floor, the coil up, the store down; add them as complex numbers and then take the size, √(R² + (ωL − 1/(ωC))²)","w":["it should read |Z| = R · ωL · 1/(ωC)","it is right at every pace but resonance","it should read |Z| = R − ωL − 1/(ωC)"],"why":"The size of a sum is at most the sum of the sizes."});
/* end phase F und_r26 */
/* phase F und_r27: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27·phys.1.1","q":"What is the turning push τ = r × F as a vector, and what does the any-pivot technique say?","a":"it points along the axis of the turning, has size rF sin θ, and points the way the right thumb does when the fingers curl with the push; if the forces on a body add to nothing, their turning push is the same about every point, so a body at rest may be balanced about any point you choose","w":["it points along the force and has size rF; the turning push is 0 about every point","it points along r and has size F sin θ; a body at rest must be balanced about its centre only","it points along the axis with size rF cos θ; the turning push about a point depends on the point even when the forces add to nothing"],"why":"Moving the pivot by Q changes τ by Q × ΣF, which is 0."},
 {"u":"27·phys.2.1","q":"What is the angular momentum L, and what does the kept-spin technique say?","a":"L = r × mv, the spin of the body about O; pulled only toward O it keeps L, since L′ = r × F = 0, so it moves in one plane and the line from O to it sweeps equal areas in equal times","w":["L = mv; a body pulled toward a point keeps its speed","L = r · mv; a body pulled toward a point moves in a circle","L = r × mv; it is kept only when no force acts at all"],"why":"For the earth, rv is the same at its nearest and farthest."},
 {"u":"27·phys.3.1","q":"What is the rotational inertia I, what does the spin-energy technique say, and why does a ball beat a disc, and a disc a hoop, down a ramp?","a":"I = Σmρ², each piece’s mass times the square of its distance from the axis; a solid turning at ω has energy ½Iω² and spin Iω; the hoop, with its mass far out, spends half its energy on turning, the disc a third, the ball only two sevenths, whatever their masses and sizes","w":["I = Σmρ; the energy is Iω; the heaviest body wins the race","I = Σmρ²; the energy is ½Iω²; the hoop wins because its I is largest","I is the mass; the energy is ½Mv² alone; all three arrive together"],"why":"Less energy in turning leaves more for moving."},
 {"u":"27·phys.4.1","q":"What is the inertia grid Θ, and what does the principal-axis technique say?","a":"the grid that takes the turning ω to the spin, L = Θω; Θ equals its own turn on its side, so its stretches are real and stretched vectors with different stretches are square to each other; spun about one of these axes the spin points along the turning, and about any other it does not","w":["the grid that takes the spin to the energy; its stretches are complex; the spin always points along the turning","the grid L = Θω; its stretches are all equal; a body has one principal axis","the grid of the body’s masses; it cannot be straightened out; the spin is square to the turning about every axis"],"why":"A wheel out of balance shakes its axle: the axle is no principal axis."},
 {"u":"27·phys.5.1","q":"What does the middle-axis technique say, and why does a wobble about the middle axis grow?","a":"a free body spun about its axis of largest or smallest rotational inertia keeps spinning about it, and about the middle one it flips; there the two differences (I₃ − I₁)(I₁ − I₂) have one sign, so the wobble obeys a rule with a real rate s and grows like e^(st), where for the other axes it only swings","w":["a free body spins steadily about every principal axis; no wobble grows","a free body flips about the largest axis; there the wobble has a complex rate","a free body flips about the middle axis because gravity turns it about its centre"],"why":"A book tossed about the axis across its strings."},
 {"u":"27·phys.6.1","q":"What does the sideways-push technique say, and why does the push do no work?","a":"seen from the turning earth a body moving at v is pushed by −2mΩ × v, in the northern half to the right of level motion, by 2Ωv sin(latitude) for each kilogram; a cross with v is square to v, so it bends the path and changes no speed","w":["the push is −2mΩ · v, along the motion, so it slows the body","the push is to the left in the northern half and grows toward the equator; it does work on the air","the push is mΩ × r, the same for a body at rest; it does no work because it is small"],"why":"Storms in the northern half turn counter-clockwise."},
 {"u":"27·phys.7.1","q":"What does the fixed-vector technique say, and what does it pay that 18·phys held in trust?","a":"under a pull toward a point that falls off as the square of the distance, the vector p × L − km r/|r| is kept, so every path is |r| = ℓ/(1 + e cos θ): an ellipse with the pulling point at a focus when e < 1, a parabola at e = 1, a hyperbola when e > 1; it pays that the planets’ paths are ellipses","w":["under any pull toward a point every path is a circle; it pays the equal areas","the kept vector is L itself; so every path is an ellipse with the pulling point at its centre","the kept vector is p; so every path is a straight line bent by the sun"],"why":"For the earth e ≈ 0.0167."},
 {"u":"27·phys.8.1","q":"What is wrong with “the spin points along the turning”, and with “a planet moves fastest where it is farthest”?","a":"L = Θω points along ω only when ω lies on a principal axis; and rv is the same at both ends of the path, so the planet is fastest where it is nearest","w":["the spin is always square to the turning; and a planet’s speed never changes","the spin points against the turning; and a planet is fastest halfway between","the first is right for every solid; the second is right since far paths are longer"],"why":"The earth: 30.29 km/s at its nearest, 29.29 at its farthest."});
/* end phase F und_r27 */
/* phase F und_r28: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["28·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["28·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"28·phys.1.1","q":"What does the stream technique say, and why does the pressure drop where the stream speeds up?","a":"along a stream of a steady fluid with no friction, pressure plus ½ρ times the square of the speed plus ρg times the height stays the same; at one height, a larger speed term leaves less for the pressure: the pressure behind pushed the fluid faster","w":["pressure times speed stays the same; the pressure drops because the tube is narrower","pressure plus speed stays the same in any fluid, friction or not; the pressure rises where the stream speeds up","the speed stays the same along a stream; the pressure drops with height only"],"why":"A jet through a tight heart valve: the speed read by ultrasound gives the pressure lost."},
 {"u":"28·phys.2.1","q":"What does the narrow-tube technique say, and where do the four powers of R come from?","a":"a steady flow through a tube goes as Q = πR⁴Δp/(8μL), so halving the radius cuts the flow at the same push to a sixteenth; two powers count the area, and the drag at the wall gives the other two","w":["the flow goes as R², the area; halving the radius gives a quarter","the flow goes as R⁴; all four powers come from the area","the flow goes as R³; two powers from the area and one from the length"],"why":"A measured radius’s share of error comes out four times as large."},
 {"u":"28·phys.3.1","q":"What does the kept-circulation technique say, and why is the circulation round a moving loop kept?","a":"in a fluid with no friction and fixed density, pushed only by pressure and gravity, the circulation round any loop moving with the fluid stays the same; those pushes are uphill from something, and an uphill field totals 0 round a closed loop, so nothing changes the circulation","w":["the circulation of every fluid dies away; friction spends it","the circulation is kept only round loops at rest; moving loops gain it","the circulation grows with the push of gravity; pressure keeps it in check"],"why":"A ring that shrinks keeps 2πr times its speed: its speed grows as 1/r."},
 {"u":"28·phys.4.1","q":"What does the enclosed-charge technique say, and why does all the charge of a metal at rest sit on its skin?","a":"the field’s flux out through any closed surface is the charge inside it over ε₀; in a metal at rest the field inside is 0, or charge would move, so every closed surface inside has flux 0 and holds no charge","w":["the flux through a closed surface is always 0; charge spreads evenly through a metal","the flux out is the charge outside the surface; charge sits at the centre of a metal","the field’s flux is the charge times ε₀; charge sits on the skin because it is lighter there"],"why":"What is inside shows on the boundary."},
 {"u":"28·phys.5.1","q":"What does the wire-field technique say, and from which measured law does it come?","a":"a long straight current I makes a field μ₀I/(2πr) round it, falling off as one over the distance; it comes from the measured law that the circulation of the field round a loop is μ₀ times the current through it, used on a circle of radius r","w":["the field is μ₀I/(4πr²), falling off as the square; from the enclosed-charge technique","the field is μ₀I, the same at every distance; from the kept-circulation technique","the field points along the wire and is μ₀I r; from the stream technique"],"why":"1000 A at 15 m makes about 13 microtesla."},
 {"u":"28·phys.6.1","q":"What is wrong with “half the radius, a quarter of the flow”?","a":"a quarter counts the area only; the drag at the wall gives two more powers, so half the radius gives a sixteenth of the flow at the same push","w":["half the radius gives half the flow","half the radius gives an eighth of the flow","nothing; the flow goes as the area"],"why":"Q goes as R⁴."});
/* end phase F und_r28 */
/* phase F und_r29: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["29·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["29·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"29·phys.1.1","q":"What does the light-clock technique say, and why is it time itself that runs slow, not one kind of clock?","a":"a clock moving at speed v ticks slower, seen from the ground, by the factor γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²); any other clock riding beside the light clock must keep step with it, or the riders could tell they were moving, so the slowing belongs to time","w":["a moving clock ticks faster by γ; only light clocks are affected","a moving clock ticks slower by 1 − v/c; it is a fault of the mirrors","a moving clock ticks slower by γ; clocks of other kinds keep ground time"],"why":"The light’s path is longer seen from the ground, and its speed is the same."},
 {"u":"29·phys.2.1","q":"What does the kept-interval technique say, and what does the mixing-turn technique say?","a":"the change to an observer moving at v keeps c²t² − x², so every steady observer reads the same ruler of spacetime, ds² = c²dt² − dx²; and that change is a turn of spacetime by u, with tanh u = v/c, two such turns adding their u","w":["the change keeps c²t² + x²; it is a turn by the angle v/c","the change keeps t and x separately; turns do not add","the change keeps ct − x; it is a slide by v, and slides add their v"],"why":"The ruler is chosen by measurement."},
 {"u":"29·phys.3.1","q":"What does the speeds-add technique say, and why can no adding of speeds reach c?","a":"two speeds along one line combine to (v₁ + v₂)/(1 + v₁v₂/c²); as turns the u add, and tanh of any u is below 1, so the speed stays below c however many are added","w":["speeds add plainly, v₁ + v₂, up to c and stop there","two speeds combine to v₁v₂/c; it never reaches c because both are below c","two speeds combine to (v₁ + v₂)/2; the average is below c"],"why":"Two speeds of 0.6c give 0.882c."},
 {"u":"29·phys.4.1","q":"What does the mass-energy technique say, and what is kept in a collision?","a":"a body of mass m carries energy γmc², which at rest is mc² and whose next term is ½mv²; the total energy and the total momentum γmv are kept, so mass can turn into other energy","w":["a body carries energy mc² whether moving or not; mass is kept in a collision","a body carries energy ½mv² only; mv is kept exactly","a body carries energy γmc; energy is kept but momentum is not"],"why":"A reactor’s fuel ends the day lighter by a few grams."},
 {"u":"29·phys.5.1","q":"What does the scattered-light technique say?","a":"light scattered by a free electron through a turn θ comes out longer in wavelength by (h/mc)(1 − cos θ), about 2.43 trillionths of a metre at a right angle, whatever the light was","w":["scattered light comes out shorter in wavelength, by an amount that grows with its colour","scattered light keeps its wavelength; only its direction changes","the shift is (h/mc) cos θ, largest straight ahead"],"why":"Light has momentum E/c, and hands some to the electron."},
 {"u":"29·phys.6.1","q":"What is wrong with “0.6c + 0.6c = 1.2c”?","a":"speeds do not add plainly: (0.6c + 0.6c)/(1 + 0.36) ≈ 0.882c; it is the turns u that add","w":["the sum is exactly c","the sum is 0.6c, since speeds do not add at all","nothing, for speeds along one line"],"why":"ln 2 + ln 2 = ln 4, and tanh(ln 4) = 15/17."},
 {"u":"29·phys.7.1","q":"What is a nucleus’s missing mass, what does the missing-mass technique say it is worth, where does the hold per particle peak, and why does it fall away on both sides?","a":"a nucleus weighs less than its protons and neutrons apart by Δm, and the energy that holds it together is Δm c²; the hold per particle peaks near iron, at about 8.8 MeV; small nuclei have many particles at the surface, with few neighbours, and in large ones the push between the many protons reaches across the whole nucleus","w":["a nucleus weighs more than its parts by Δm; the hold peaks at uranium","the missing mass is the mass of its electrons; the hold is the same for every nucleus","the missing mass is worth Δm c; the hold peaks at hydrogen and falls from there"],"why":"The grip reaches only neighbours; the push reaches everyone."},
 {"u":"29·phys.8.1","q":"Why do both splitting a heavy nucleus and joining two light ones give out energy, and what does the toward-iron technique say about the sun?","a":"a change that keeps the count of protons and neutrons gives out the mass it loses times c², which is the gain in total hold, and both roads lead toward iron, where the hold per particle is greatest; the sun joins hydrogen into helium and loses about four million tonnes of mass each second","w":["splitting gives out energy and joining takes it in; the sun splits heavy nuclei","both give out energy because both destroy particles; the sun loses no mass","joining gives out energy at every size; the sun gains mass as it burns"],"why":"Joining is harder to start: the nuclei must come close against their push apart."});
/* end phase F und_r29 */
/* phase F und_r30: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30·phys.1.1","q":"What does the energy-share technique say, and what do the lines-up and log-bounds techniques each contribute?","a":"among all ways to share out a fixed total of energy, the shares with the most ways give each state a share in proportion to e^(−E/kT); the lines-up technique finds the shares, and the log-bounds technique proves that they truly have the most ways, not merely a level point","w":["each state gets an equal share; lines-up proves it and log-bounds finds the temperature","each state gets a share in proportion to E/kT; log-bounds finds the shares and lines-up proves them","each state gets a share in proportion to e^(−E/kT); lines-up alone proves that these have the most ways"],"why":"The most ways are the most likely by far."},
 {"u":"30·phys.2.1","q":"What does the sum-over-states technique say Z holds?","a":"the average energy is minus the slope of ln Z along β, and the spread of the energy is the next slope, which equals kT² times the heat capacity; every quantity of the body’s heat can be read from Z","w":["Z is the average energy, and its slope is the temperature","the average energy is ln Z, and the energy has no spread","the average energy is minus the slope of ln Z along β; the spread of the energy cannot be read from Z"],"why":"The heat a body soaks up and the jitter of its energy are one number."},
 {"u":"30·phys.3.1","q":"What are the three ways to hold a body, and what does the grand-share technique say for the third?","a":"at a fixed energy, trading energy with a room, and trading energy and molecules with a room; in the third the body shares itself out in proportion to e^(−(E − μN)/kT), with μ the room’s chemical potential","w":["hot, cold and at rest; in the third every state has an equal share","at a fixed energy, at a fixed volume and at a fixed pressure; in the third the shares go as e^(−E/kT)","at a fixed energy, trading energy, and trading energy and molecules; in the third the shares go as e^(−(E + μN)/kT)"],"why":"A site that holds one oxygen molecule or none is such a body."},
 {"u":"30·phys.4.1","q":"What does the half-kT technique say, and why is it called equipartition?","a":"every part of the energy that goes as a square averages ½kT, whatever its size, so a body’s heat capacity counts its squares, ½k for each; the energy is parted equally among the squares","w":["every molecule holds ½kT in all; the molecules share equally","every part of the energy averages kT; the parts are equal in size","every square averages ½kT only for motion along a line; turning and shaking take none"],"why":"Here it comes from the energy-share technique, and covers turning and shaking as well."},
 {"u":"30·phys.5.1","q":"What do the states-multiply and frozen-shake techniques say?","a":"when a molecule’s energy is a sum of independent parts, its sum over states is the product of each part’s, so ln Z and the average energies add; a shake that takes energy in lumps hf holds about kT when kT is far above hf, and almost nothing when kT is far below it","w":["the sums over states add, so ln Z multiplies; a shake always holds kT","the sum over states is the product of each part’s; a shake holds almost nothing when kT is far above hf","the sums over states multiply; a shake holds ½kT at every temperature"],"why":"Nitrogen at 293 K: about 9 molecules in a million shake at all."},
 {"u":"30·phys.6.1","q":"Why is “the lines-up shares are the most ways” a move to watch out for?","a":"the lines-up technique finds only where the count of ways is level, which could be a top, a bottom or neither; that it is the most needs the second check, by the log-bounds technique","w":["the lines-up shares are the fewest ways","the lines-up shares are the most ways only at high temperature","nothing is wrong; a level point of the count is always its top"],"why":"A level point is only a candidate for the top."},
 {"u":"30·phys.7.1","q":"How is μ the slope of G, what does the downhill-μ technique say, and why does oxygen run from the lungs into the blood?","a":"μ is the slope of G along the number of moles of a kind, and per molecule it is that divided by the count of molecules in a mole; molecules move from the part where their μ is higher to the part where it is lower and rest when the two are equal; oxygen’s μ is higher in the air of the lungs than in the blood arriving there","w":["μ is G per unit of volume; molecules move toward higher μ; oxygen’s μ is higher in the blood","μ is the slope of G along temperature; molecules rest when their counts are equal; the lungs hold more oxygen molecules","μ is the slope of G along moles; molecules move from lower μ to higher; oxygen is pumped against its μ"],"why":"Moving dn moles downhill in μ lowers G."},
 {"u":"30·phys.8.1","q":"Why does salt lower the μ of the water it is dissolved in, what does the solvent-push technique say, and why does a red cell burst in pure water?","a":"the salt lowers the water’s share x_w, and μ falls with ln x_w; a dilute solution with c moles of solute particles per unit of volume draws water through a skin that stops the solute until it stands at an extra pressure Π = cRT; the water outside the cell has the higher μ and runs in until the skin gives way","w":["the salt heats the water; Π = cR/T; the cell bursts because salt runs out","the salt raises the water’s μ; Π = cRT pushes water out of the solution; the cell bursts because it dries","the salt lowers the water’s share; Π = cRT; the cell bursts because the salt inside expands"],"why":"μ_w = μ_w* + RT ln x_w + vΠ."});
/* end phase F und_r30 */
/* phase F und_r31: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31·phys.1.1","q":"What does the filter-shadow technique say, why does doing the filter twice change nothing, and why does sunlight pass any filter at one half?","a":"a filter with line d acts by the shadow map P = ddᵀ and keeps |E|² cos²θ of the brightness; PP = d(dᵀd)dᵀ = P because |d| = 1; sunlight's line of shaking takes every angle alike, and the average of cos² over all angles is ½","w":["a filter dims by a fixed share; twice dims twice; sunlight passes at ½ because half its colours are stopped","a filter turns the shaking to its line and keeps all the brightness; twice turns twice; sunlight passes whole","a filter keeps |E| cos θ of the brightness; twice squares it; sunlight has θ = 45°"],"why":"What is already along d has no part left to lose."},
 {"u":"31·phys.2.1","q":"What does the stack technique say about the light that leaves a row of filters, and when do two filters give the same result in either order?","a":"E leaves as (E · d₁)(d₁ · d₂)⋯(dₖ₋₁ · dₖ)dₖ, along the last line; two filters' maps agree in either order only when their lines are the same or at right angles","w":["the light leaves along the first line; order never matters","the light leaves along the average of the lines; order matters only for three filters","the light leaves along the last line with all its brightness; order matters only at right angles"],"why":"A middle filter lets through part of what a crossed pair throws away whole."},
 {"u":"31·phys.3.1","q":"What does the level-glare technique say, which fact does it hold in trust, and why is the line of polarized sunglasses upright?","a":"when the sun's rays meet water of index n at the angle from straight down with tan θ = n, the reflected light shakes only level; held in trust: a shaking charge sends no light along its own line of shaking; an upright line keeps cos² of 90° of level light, which is nothing","w":["reflected light shakes only upright; in trust: water is flat; an upright line keeps all the glare","reflected light does not shake; in trust: tan θ = n; the line is upright so the glasses sit level","glare shakes at 45°; in trust: sunlight is one vector; the line is upright to pass the glare"],"why":"For water, n ≈ 1.33 and the angle is about 53°."},
 {"u":"31·phys.4.1","q":"Why does sunlight through one filter keep one half and not cos²θ, and why can no filter undo what an earlier filter took out?","a":"sunlight has no one θ: its line wanders through every angle, and the average of cos² is ½; and a shadow map has determinant 0: the part across its line is gone, and no later map brings it back","w":["sunlight has θ = 45° exactly; a second filter at the same line undoes the first","sunlight keeps cos²θ with θ the sun's height; filters can be undone by turning them over","one half is a rounding; a filter is undone by a filter at right angles"],"why":"A shadow map is never undone."});
/* end phase F und_r31 */
/* phase F und_r32: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["32·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["32·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"32·phys.1.1","q":"Which frequencies can a string held at both ends make, what does the pluck-point technique say, and how does a bowed string differ?","a":"the whole multiples n f₁ of f₁ = v/(2L); plucked at d, the nth amount goes as sin(nπd/L)/n², so the amounts fall like 1/n² and every shape still at the pluck point is missing; a bowed string pushes like a sawtooth, with amounts falling like 1/n, richer in high shapes","w":["every frequency above f₁; plucked at d the amounts fall like 1/n and none is missing; a bowed string falls like 1/n²","the whole multiples of f₁; plucked at d the amounts are all equal; a bowed string holds f₁ alone","the odd multiples of f₁ only; plucked at d the shapes with a peak at d are missing; a bowed string is the same"],"why":"A corner gives 1/n², a jump 1/n (32h3)."},
 {"u":"32·phys.2.1","q":"What does the body-filter technique say, and why do the body’s peaks stay put for every note?","a":"the note’s recipe at the ear is the string’s recipe multiplied, speed by speed, by the body’s gain H(nf₁); the gain belongs to the body, a function of frequency alone, so its peaks stay while the notes move under them","w":["the note’s recipe is the string’s recipe plus the body’s; the peaks move up with the note","the body adds new frequencies the string does not hold; the peaks sit at whole multiples of the note","the body scales every speed by one number; it has no peaks"],"why":"The body is a blend, in the sense of 32i."},
 {"u":"32·phys.3.1","q":"What does the beat technique say, and how are beats used to tune a string?","a":"two equal turnings at f₁ and f₂ add to a turning at the middle frequency (f₁ + f₂)/2 whose size swells and fades |f₁ − f₂| times a second; the player turns the peg until the throb slows and stops","w":["they add to a turning at f₁ + f₂ that swells (f₁ + f₂)/2 times a second; the player tunes until the throb is fastest","they add to two separate notes with no swelling; the player compares the pitches by ear","they add to a turning at |f₁ − f₂|; the player tunes until that low note is loudest"],"why":"442 Hz against 440 Hz beats twice a second."},
 {"u":"32·phys.4.1","q":"What are the crest speed and the group speed, and what does the group-speed technique give on deep water?","a":"crests move at ω/k; a bunch gathered near k₀ moves at the slope ω′(k₀), the same only when ω is in proportion to k; on deep water ω = √(gk), so bunches move at half the crest speed","w":["crests move at ω′(k) and bunches at ω/k; on deep water the bunches move at twice the crest speed","both move at ω/k for every wave; on deep water all waves move at √g","crests move at ω/k and bunches at ω′(k₀); on a string the two differ, and on deep water they agree"],"why":"The envelope depends on x − ω′(k₀)t."},
 {"u":"32·phys.5.1","q":"Why is “two instruments playing the same 440 Hz note make the same wave” a move to watch out for?","a":"the same note means the same repeat; the recipes, the amounts of each whole multiple, differ with the string and the body, and so do the waves","w":["they make the same wave, only at different loudness","they differ only in the turns of their coefficients","no two instruments can share a repeat"],"why":"Same repeat, different recipes."},
 {"u":"32·phys.6.1","q":"Why does light from N slits add as a geometric sum of turnings, what does the sharp-lines technique say, and how many slits split sodium’s yellow lines?","a":"each slit’s path is longer than the last by d sin θ, a turn φ = 2πd sin θ/λ; the size is |sin(Nφ/2)/sin(φ/2)|, N at d sin θ = mλ and 0 already at mλ + λ/N, so lines narrow as 1/N and Δλ ≥ λ/(mN) is told apart; about 982 slits in the first line","w":["each slit adds the same turn; the size is N at every angle; two slits already split sodium’s lines","each slit’s turn is φ more than the last; the lines narrow as 1/√N; about 982² slits are needed","each slit’s turn is φ more than the last; the lines keep the width of two slits and only brighten; no count of slits splits the lines"],"why":"589.3/0.6 ≈ 982."});
/* end phase F und_r32 */
/* phase F und_r34: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["34·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["34·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"34·phys.1.1","q":"What does the wrap-count technique say, and why does the flux not change while a surface slides without crossing a charge?","a":"sliding a closed surface through space, never across a charge, never changes the flux of the field out of it; the change in flux is the total of the spread of E over the region swept, and the spread is 0 wherever there is no charge","w":["a larger surface has more flux; the flux grows with the area swept","the flux changes smoothly as a surface nears a charge; the spread of E is never 0","the flux is the same for every surface whatever it holds; charges do not enter"],"why":"Counted, not measured: only which charges it holds."},
 {"u":"34·phys.2.1","q":"What is the potential of a field of charges at rest, what rule does it obey where there is no charge, and what does the no-trap technique say?","a":"a height V with E = −∇V; where there is no charge V is balanced, its value at a point the average over every small sphere about it; so V has no lowest and no highest point inside such a region, and fixed charges cannot hold a charge in stable rest","w":["V with E = ∇V; V obeys the heat rule; a ring of fixed charges traps a charge at its centre","V = |E|; V is constant where there is no charge; no field can exist there","V with E = −∇V; V is the largest value on each sphere; a charge rests stably at every point"],"why":"An average equal to its least value is constant."},
 {"u":"34·phys.3.1","q":"What does the far-field technique say, what is the pair moment, and why is water’s field drop so large?","a":"far from a cluster its potential is KQ/R + K(p · r̂)/R² plus terms that fall faster, with Q the total charge and p = Σ qᵢrᵢ the pair moment; a water molecule has Q = 0 and a large p, and in a field the molecules turn to line their pair moments up, their own fields cancelling most of it: κ about 80","w":["far away the potential is KQ/R² and nothing more; p is the product of the charges; water’s κ is large because water is dense","far away the potential is KQ/R + Kp/R; p = Σ qᵢ; water’s κ is large because its molecules carry a net charge","a neutral cluster has no field at all; p is its radius; water’s κ is about 2, like oil’s"],"why":"That is why salt dissolves in water and not in oil."},
 {"u":"34·phys.4.1","q":"What does a changing magnetic flux do round a loop, what does the missing-term technique say, and why is the term required?","a":"it gives a circulation of E round the loop equal to minus the rate of change of the flux of B through it; the steady law, swirl of B = μ₀J, cannot hold where charge piles up, and with μ₀ε₀ ∂E/∂t added it keeps charge and reduces to the old law for steady currents; the spread of a swirl is 0, so the spread of the right side must be 0 too","w":["it makes a steady current with no field; the missing term is μ₀J itself; it was measured first on a bench","it gives a circulation of B; the missing term is −∂B/∂t; it is required by the kept energy","it changes nothing round a loop; the missing term is a matter of taste, required by no law"],"why":"It was required by logic before it could be measured."},
 {"u":"34·phys.5.1","q":"What does the light-speed technique say, from which two measured constants does c come, and why does a light wave’s field shake across its direction of travel?","a":"in empty space each entry of E and of B obeys the wave rule with speed 1/√(μ₀ε₀) = 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s; μ₀ comes from the push between currents and ε₀ from the push between charges; spread of E = 0 leaves the wave’s field no entry along its direction of travel","w":["E and B obey the heat rule with rate μ₀ε₀; both constants come from the speed of light; the field shakes along the travel","each entry obeys the wave rule with speed μ₀ε₀; the constants are measured with light; the field shakes across because swirl of E = 0","E alone obeys the wave rule; c comes from the earth’s field; the field does not shake"],"why":"Bench measurements with wires and coils give the speed of light."},
 {"u":"34·phys.6.1","q":"What does the shaken-charge technique say, and why does it make the sky blue?","a":"a charge shaken as x₀ cos ωt sends out on average q²ω⁴x₀²/(12πε₀c³) of power, so twice the pace sends sixteen times the light; sunlight shakes the charges of the air, and they send the fast blue shakes sideways far more strongly than the slow red ones","w":["a shaken charge sends power in proportion to ω, so blue is sent twice as strongly as red, straight ahead","a charge moving steadily sends power q²v²; the sky is blue because air absorbs red","a shaken charge sends no power; the sky is blue by reflection of the sea"],"why":"The long path at sunset has lost its blue and is red."},
 {"u":"34·phys.7.1","q":"Why is “a ring of fixed charges can hold a charge still at its centre” a move to watch out for?","a":"where there is no charge the potential has no lowest and no highest point, so some small move always lowers the energy: no stable rest","w":["the centre of a ring has no field, so the charge is trapped","a ring holds a charge only if the ring is large","fixed charges make no potential"],"why":"Real traps swing their fields, or add a magnetic field."});
/* end phase F und_r34 */
/* phase F und_r35: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["35·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["35·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"35·phys.1.1","q":"What does the matter-wave law give a body with momentum p, what measurement bore it out, and why is a thrown baseball’s wave never seen?","a":"It behaves as a wave of wavelength h/p; electrons of 54 eV came off a crystal strongest near 50°, where d sin θ = λ puts the first bright band at about 51°; the ball’s wavelength, about 10⁻³⁴ m, is far below the width of anything it could pass through","w":["A wave of wavelength p/h; it was proved from E = hf with no measurement; the ball is too slow to have a wave","A wave of wavelength h/p; electrons came off a crystal evenly in every direction; the ball has no wavelength because it is not charged","A wave of frequency h/p; light bent round the crystal; the ball’s wave is too large to see"],"why":"It is a law: guessed in 1924, then measured."},
 {"u":"35·phys.2.1","q":"What does the standing-wave technique say of a wave φ(x)e^(−iEt/ħ), and what is laid down beside the wave law to say what the wave means?","a":"It obeys the law exactly when φ is a shape of −(ħ²/2m)φ″ + Vφ = Eφ with number E, and then its size never changes; laid down beside it: |ψ|² is a density, the chance of finding the electron between x and x + dx is |ψ(x)|²dx, with total 1","w":["It obeys the law for every φ, and its size grows with time; laid down beside it: ψ is the chance of finding the electron","It obeys the law exactly when φ is a shape of the rule with number E; laid down beside it: |ψ|² says where the next electron lands","It obeys the law only when V = 0; laid down beside it: |ψ| is the electron’s speed"],"why":"The law is exact about the crowd and silent about the one."},
 {"u":"35·phys.3.1","q":"Which shapes and energies does the box technique allow an electron between walls L apart, and what does the missing n = 0 mean?","a":"Shapes √(2/L) sin(nπx/L) with energies n²h²/(8mL²) for n = 1, 2, 3, … and no others; n = 0 would be 0 everywhere, no electron to find, so a held electron can never be at rest","w":["Shapes sin(nπx/L) with energies nh²/(8mL²) for n = 0, 1, 2, …; n = 0 is the electron at rest","Shapes √(2/L) sin(nπx/L) with energies n²h²/(8mL²) for n = 1, 2, 3, …; n = 0 is left out only by custom","Shapes cos(nπx/L) with any energy at all; n = 0 is the lowest rung"],"why":"Its least energy is set by the box."},
 {"u":"35·phys.4.1","q":"How does the chance of crossing a wall higher than the electron’s energy fall with its width, why is the growing shape thrown out inside the wall, and what makes a tunnelling microscope so fine a reader of height?","a":"About as e^(−2κd); e^(κx) grows without bound, which no chance density can do, so the wave dies away as e^(−κx); one more tenth of a nanometre of gap cuts the current by a factor of about 9","w":["As 1/d; the growing shape is kept and the dying one thrown out; the tip touches each atom","About as e^(−2κd); the wave stops dead at the wall’s face; the current does not change with the gap","About as e^(−κd²); the growing shape breaks the energy law; the current doubles for each nanometre"],"why":"The electrons that cross are the ones that tunnel."},
 {"u":"35·phys.5.1","q":"Which energies does the ladder technique give a spring, what is its lowest rung, and what follows about stillness?","a":"(n + ½)ħω for n = 0, 1, 2, …, each raising move climbing one rung; the lowest rung is the bell e^(−x²/2) with energy ½ħω, not 0, so a spring can never be still","w":["nħω for n = 0, 1, 2, …; the lowest rung has energy 0, a spring at rest","(n + ½)ħω; the lowest rung is sin x with energy ħω, and a cold spring is still","n²ħω, with rungs crowding apart; the lowest rung is the bell with energy ½ħω"],"why":"H(Rφ) = (E + 1)Rφ in the units of the footing."},
 {"u":"35·phys.6.1","q":"For which r₀ is e^(−r/r₀) a shape of hydrogen’s rule, which requirement fixes it, and how does the red line of hydrogen arise?","a":"r₀ = ħ²/(mke²), about 0.0529 nm, with energy −13.6 eV; the 1/r terms must cancel, since E is one number; the red line, 656 nm, is the 1.89 eV given off in falling from n = 3 to n = 2","w":["r₀ = ħ²/(mke²) with energy +13.6 eV; fixed by the total chance being 1; the red line is the fall from n = 2 to n = 1","r₀ = 1 nm; fixed by measuring; the red line is the fall from n = 4 to n = 2","r₀ = ħ²/(mke²), about 0.0529 nm; fixed by the 1/r terms cancelling; the red line is the 13.6 eV needed to pull the electron away"],"why":"The other energies are −13.6 eV/n²."},
 {"u":"35·phys.7.1","q":"Why can ψ itself not be read as the chance of finding the electron?","a":"ψ is complex; the density is |ψ|²","w":["ψ is always negative","ψ is the chance only when V = 0","ψ is the electron’s energy"],"why":"A chance is a real number from 0 to 1."});
/* end phase F und_r35 */
