(window.LADDER_CARDS["36·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["36·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"36·phys.1.1","q":"What does the counts-add technique say of two lamps, why must their light arrive independently, and when is “together” not a sum?","a":"The amount at each wavelength of the two together is the sum of their amounts, since no photon is from both lamps and counts of separate sets add; when one beam is split and rejoined, the waves keep time, and where they meet half a turn apart they cancel: then the waves add, not the amounts","w":["The amounts multiply, wavelength by wavelength; independence makes no difference; together is never a sum","The amounts add because the lamps have the same colour; with two lamps of different colours together is not a sum","The amounts add since no photon is from both lamps; together fails only when one lamp is far brighter than the other"],"why":"Two ordinary lamps never keep time for long: a fact by cases with a rate."},
 {"u":"36·phys.2.1","q":"What does a filter do to a spectrum at each wavelength, what does the through-filters technique say, and why is a pot of mixed paint not two filters in a row?","a":"It keeps a share t(λ) between 0 and 1, so S comes out as t·S; through two filters the light is t₁t₂S, in either order; in a pot some light bounces back off grains near the top and never passes through anything","w":["It adds its own spectrum to S; two filters give S + t₁ + t₂; a pot is two filters in a row","It keeps a share t(λ); two filters give (t₁ + t₂)S, and the order matters; a pot differs because paint is thicker","It keeps a share t(λ); two filters give t₁t₂S in either order; a pot is the same product, and 36h6 says so"],"why":"Multiplying, not adding: the pigment mix seen in the light itself."},
 {"u":"36·phys.3.1","q":"Why does a molecule remove only certain wavelengths, which wavelength does the gap-to-wavelength technique give, and how sure is it?","a":"It can take a photon only when the photon’s energy fits a gap ΔE between two of its steps; that is the light at λ = hc/ΔE; a gap known to a share s gives the wavelength to the same share s","w":["It takes every photon above a threshold energy; λ = hc · ΔE; the doubt in λ is twice that in the gap","It takes a photon only when its energy fits a gap; λ = hc/ΔE; the wavelength is exact whatever the doubt in the gap","It takes photons at random; λ = ΔE/hc; a gap known to 1% gives the wavelength to 10%"],"why":"A gap of 3.40 × 10⁻¹⁹ J takes light at 584 nm, known to about 6 nm if the gap is known to 1%."},
 {"u":"36·phys.4.1","q":"What are a stove’s warmth’s three numbers and its energy, what does the unseen-light technique say, and what does a prism show of two metamers?","a":"(0, 0, 0), with energy above 0; adding light from outside the window to any light keeps its colour and adds to its energy; a prism lands each wavelength at its own place, so two metamers make two different patterns: a spike at 580 nm one line, a red and a green light two","w":["(1, 0, 0), red, with energy 0; adding it changes the colour and not the energy; a prism shows metamers as one pattern","(0, 0, 0), with energy 0; adding it changes nothing; a prism cannot tell metamers apart","(0, 0, 0), with energy above 0; adding it keeps the colour and the energy; a prism turns one metamer into the other"],"why":"The two weightings of 36b4 pulled as far apart as they go."},
 {"u":"36·phys.5.1","q":"Why do the shares sent back, passed and taken add to 1 at each wavelength, what does the pane technique give, and why is that a little more than the straight path alone?","a":"Each photon does exactly one of the three, and counts add; a clear pane whose faces each send back r and which takes nothing passes (1 − r)/(1 + r); the straight path gives (1 − r)², and light bounced back and forward inside adds r², r⁴ and so on of that","w":["The shares are defined as thirds; the pane passes (1 − r)²; nothing is added by bounces","Each photon does exactly one of the three; the pane passes 1 − r; bounces inside take light away, so it is a little less than the straight path","Energy is lost at each face; the pane passes (1 − r)/(1 + r), less than the straight path because of the bounces"],"why":"For glass r = 0.04: 0.923 passed against 0.9216 straight."},
 {"u":"36·phys.6.1","q":"How can the sun peak near 502 nm and not look green, what does the glow-colour technique say of a lamp filament, and what does 2700 K on a lamp’s box promise?","a":"A colour is col of the whole spectrum, and a peak is one place on a spread; a filament near 2700 K peaks near 1073 nm, outside the window, since λmax = 2.898 × 10⁻³/T; the box promises three numbers near col of the 2700 K glow, a metamer, not that the lamp glows at that temperature","w":["The air removes the green; a filament peaks at 502 nm; the box promises the lamp runs at 2700 K","A colour is col of the whole spectrum; a filament near 2700 K peaks near 251 nm, in the ultraviolet; the box promises a spectrum equal to the glow’s","The eye has no curve at 502 nm; a filament has no peak; the box promises 2700 photons per second"],"why":"Doubling T halves λmax."},
 {"u":"36·phys.7.1","q":"Why does a thin gas glow in spikes where a hot solid glows in a spread, what does the lines technique say of a cool layer of the same gas, and what can then be read in sunlight?","a":"Each fall between two levels of the gas gives one photon that fits one gap, so the glow is a sum of spikes at λ = hc/(E_m − E_n); a cool layer in front of a glow takes away spikes at exactly those wavelengths, its gaps being the same; so the lines missing from sunlight tell which gases the sun’s outer layer holds","w":["A thin gas is cooler than a solid; a cool layer adds lines at new wavelengths; sunlight shows the sun’s temperature only","Each fall gives one photon that fits one gap; a cool layer of the same gas lets those wavelengths through and takes all others; sunlight shows no lines","A thin gas has one level; a cool layer shifts every line toward the red; the lines tell the sun’s distance"],"why":"Hydrogen: 656.4, 486.2 and 434.1 nm among its lines in the window."});
/* end phase F und_r36 */
/* phase F und_r37: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["37·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37·phys.1.1","q":"Why is air stiffer under a sound’s quick squeeze than under a slow one, and what does the warm-air technique give for the speed of sound at 35 °C?","a":"The squeeze is too quick for its heat to leak away: the air warms a little and the warmth pushes back, so the stiffness is larger by the factor γ = 1.4; c = 331.3 √(308.15/273.15), about 352 m/s","w":["Quick squeezes make the air denser; about 310 m/s, since warm air is slower","The warmth of a quick squeeze pushes back, making the stiffness γ times larger; about 331 m/s, the same at every temperature","A slow squeeze lets air escape; about 366 m/s, 35 m/s above freezing"],"why":"About 0.6 m/s more for every degree."},
 {"u":"37·phys.2.1","q":"What does the moving-listener technique say, and what pitch does a cyclist hear riding at 10 m/s away from a still 500 Hz bell?","a":"A listener moving towards a still source at v hears it (c + v)/c times as often, and moving away (c − v)/c; 500 × 333/343, about 485 Hz","w":["Moving towards, c/(c − v) times as often, the same as a moving source; about 515 Hz","Moving away, (c − v)/c times as often; about 515 Hz","Moving away, (c − v)/c times as often; about 490 Hz, 10 Hz lower for 10 m/s"],"why":"Crests c/f apart arrive at relative speed c − v."},
 {"u":"37·phys.3.1","q":"What does the sound-cone technique say, why is the boom not one event, and what is the half-angle for a bullet at 686 m/s?","a":"A source faster than sound trails a cone of sound whose half-angle θ has sin θ = c/v; the cone is dragged along for as long as the source is faster than sound; for 686 m/s, sin θ = ½, so 30°","w":["sin θ = v/c; the boom happens once, at the moment the speed of sound is passed; 60°","sin θ = c/v; the boom is dragged along; 60°, since 686 is twice 343","tan θ = c/v; the boom is made once; about 27°"],"why":"The touching line is at right angles to the sphere’s radius ct, a distance vt from its centre."},
 {"u":"37·phys.4.1","q":"What does the bottle technique say, what plays the mass and what the spring, and what does a 119 Hz bottle hum when only a quarter of its volume is left as air?","a":"A bottle hums at f = (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the plug of air in the neck is the mass and the air in the body the spring; a quarter of the volume doubles the note: about 238 Hz","w":["It hums at c/(4L), like a tube; the glass is the mass; about 476 Hz","It hums at (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the neck’s air is the mass and the body’s the spring; about 60 Hz, since less air hums lower","It hums at (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the drink is the mass; about 137 Hz"],"why":"f goes as 1/√V, whatever the bottle’s shape."},
 {"u":"37·phys.5.1","q":"“A jet makes its boom when it breaks the sound barrier.” What does the sound-cone technique say instead?","a":"The cone is trailed for as long as the jet flies faster than sound, and each place on the ground hears the crack as the cone sweeps over it","w":["The boom is made once, and its echo is what later listeners hear","The boom is made only when the jet slows back below the speed of sound","There is no boom unless sin θ = 1"],"why":"sin θ = c/v at every moment with v > c."});
/* end phase F und_r37 */
