(window.LADDER_CARDS["38·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38·phys.1.1","q":"What is spin, why can a swap of two electrons change their joint state only by +1 or −1, what does the swap-sign technique say, and why can two electrons still share an orbital?","a":"A vector in a space with two directions, up and down, carried beside the wave in space; electrons have no labels, so a swap can only multiply ψ by a factor, and two swaps change nothing, so the factor squared is 1; if the joint state changes sign under a swap, two electrons in the same one-electron state give the zero state; φ↑ and φ↓ are different one-electron states","w":["A turning of the electron about itself with any number of directions; a swap changes nothing at all; two electrons in one state double the state; they cannot share an orbital","A vector with two directions; the factor squared is 1; two electrons in the same state give the zero state; so no two electrons can share an orbital","A vector with two directions; the factor is always +1; two electrons in the same state repel; they share an orbital by taking turns"],"why":"The exclusion rule is about which joint states exist."},
 {"u":"38·phys.2.1","q":"What does the shell-count technique say, which two facts give it, and why do the rows of the table not simply follow 2n²?","a":"Level n of hydrogen’s orbitals holds 2n² electrons; the orbitals of level n number 1 + 3 + … + (2n − 1) = n², and each takes two spins by the swap-sign technique; in larger atoms the inner electrons screen the nucleus more for some shapes than others, so the shapes of one level no longer share an energy and the order of filling shifts: rows of 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32","w":["Level n holds n² electrons; one to each orbital; the rows follow n² exactly","Level n holds 2n² electrons; n² orbitals and two spins; the rows differ because heavy nuclei lose charge","Level n holds 2n electrons; n orbitals and two spins; the rows differ because spin is lost in large atoms"],"why":"Closed shells fall at 2, 10, 18, 36, 54 and 86."},
 {"u":"38·phys.3.1","q":"What does the upper-bound technique say, what in rung 31 does its proof use, and what two estimates of helium’s energy are made, with which one sure to lie above the truth?","a":"The average energy in any trial state is at least the lowest energy; the energy is a symmetric map, so by rung 31 it has perpendicular axes and the average is a weighted mean of the levels Eᵢ; first, the old state plus the average push, −108.8 + 34.0 = −74.8 eV; second, a trial charge Z′ = 27/16 giving −77.5 eV, a bound from above by the technique; measured −79.0 eV","w":["The average energy in a trial state is at most the lowest energy; so −77.5 eV lies below the truth","The average energy in any trial state is at least the lowest; the two estimates are −108.8 and −79.0 eV; the first is the bound","The average energy equals the lowest for the best trial state; so −77.5 eV is helium’s energy exactly"],"why":"A trial state can only overshoot."},
 {"u":"38·phys.4.1","q":"What does the two-shares technique say for each sign, and why do the two shares agree at high energy?","a":"A state of energy E holds on average 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) + 1) particles of the sign −1, which sit 0 or 1 to a state, and 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) − 1) of the sign +1, which sit in any number; at high energy the exponential is large, the ±1 beside it hardly counts, and both become e^(−(E − μ)/kT): states mostly empty do not show the sign","w":["Sign −1 has the − 1 below and sign +1 the + 1; they agree at low energy","Both signs give 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) + 1); they agree everywhere","Sign −1 gives + 1 below, sign +1 gives − 1; they agree at high energy because every state is full there"],"why":"At low energy they part: electrons stack up one to a state, light piles in."},
 {"u":"38·phys.5.1","q":"What does the glow technique say, which three counts multiply to give it, and why does the total grow as T⁴?","a":"The energy per unit volume per unit frequency is 8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1); the states per unit frequency, 8πν²/c³, times the energy hν of a lump, times the share 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1) of sign +1 with μ = 0; put x = hν/kT in the total and it is T⁴ times a number free of T","w":["8πν²/c³ times kT; states times kT alone; the total grows as T","8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) + 1); states, energy and the share of sign −1; the total grows as T⁴ because light has four states","8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1); states, energy and share; the total grows as T⁴ because each lump’s energy grows as T⁴"],"why":"ν³ dν gives four powers of T under x = hν/kT."},
 {"u":"38·phys.6.1","q":"Why is “two electrons cannot share an orbital” not the exclusion rule, and why is −77.5 eV not helium’s energy?","a":"They can share an orbital with different spins; what they cannot share is one state; and −77.5 eV is a bound from above, with the measured value −79.0 eV below it","w":["They can never share an orbital; −77.5 eV is a bound from below","They can share with different spins; and −77.5 eV is the measured value, −79.0 the estimate","They can share one state when cold; −77.5 eV leaves out the spins"],"why":"The swap-sign and upper-bound techniques, read with care."},
 {"u":"38·phys.7.1","q":"What is an outer electron’s effective charge and how is it read from a measured energy, what does the screened-charge technique say, and why does the pull fall down a column?","a":"The charge it feels through the electrons nearer in, read as Z_eff = n√(I/13.6 eV); if each inner electron cancels a whole charge and each fellow of the same level only a share σ below 1, Z_eff rises by 1 − σ with each element along a row, and the pull with it; down a column a whole closed level screens the added charges almost entirely, so Z_eff creeps from 1.26 to 3.21 while n grows from 2 to 6: caesium 3.89 eV against lithium 5.39","w":["The whole charge Z; read as Z = I/13.6; it rises by 1 along a row; the pull rises down a column","The charge it feels, Z_eff = n√(I/13.6 eV); it rises by 1 − σ along a row; down a column Z_eff falls to zero","The charge it feels, Z_eff = √(I/13.6 eV)/n; it falls by σ along a row; down a column n shrinks"],"why":"I = 13.6 (Z_eff/n)²: up along a row, down a column."},
 {"u":"38·phys.8.1","q":"What does the shell-reading technique say, which premise sets the sizes of the peaks, and what do neon’s three peaks show?","a":"In the counts of electrons knocked out by light of one energy, each level and shape gives one peak at the energy that holds it; if each electron is as likely as any other to take the light, the sizes stand in the ratio of the numbers of electrons; neon’s peaks at 870.6, 48.5 and 21.6 eV with sizes 2 : 2 : 6 show level 1, then level 2 with ℓ = 0 and with ℓ = 1, and a gap of more than 800 eV between the levels","w":["Each electron gives its own peak; sizes follow the energies; neon shows ten peaks","Each level and shape gives one peak; the sizes follow the holding energy; neon’s peaks show sizes 2 : 8","Each level gives one peak; equal chances set the sizes; neon’s two peaks show 2 and 8 with no split by shape"],"why":"The shell-count technique read off a counter."},
 {"u":"38·phys.9.1","q":"What does the small-push technique say, where does its proof use that the rule is a symmetric map, and when is the shared-level technique needed?","a":"If the level E₀ of H₀ has a single shape u₀ of size 1, a small push λV moves it to E₀ + λ⟨u₀, Vu₀⟩ to first order: by the average of the push in the old shape; the proof moves H₀ across the product, ⟨u₀, H₀w⟩ = ⟨H₀u₀, w⟩ = E₀⟨u₀, w⟩, so the unknown w drops out; when a level has several shapes the push can mix them, and the level splits by the stretches of the grid of averages ⟨uᵢ, Vuⱼ⟩","w":["The level moves by λ times the largest value of V; symmetry is not used; the shared-level technique is for large pushes","The level moves by the average of the push in the old shape; symmetry is used to make ⟨u₀, u₀⟩ = 1; the shared-level technique is for levels with no shape","The level does not move to first order; symmetry cancels the push; several shapes each move by the same average"],"why":"Helium’s first estimate was this rule’s first term."},
 {"u":"38·phys.10.1","q":"Why does hydrogen’s lowest level not move, to first order, in an electric field while level 2 splits, what does the magnet-shift technique say, and how far apart are the three lines in a magnet of 2 T?","a":"In 1s the electron is as likely at z as at −z, so the average of eFz is 0; 2s and 2p₀ share a level and the field mixes them, a grid with stretches ±3eFr₀; in a magnet B a level with turning m moves by m μ_B B, so a line from ℓ = 1 to ℓ = 0, spins set aside, splits into three spaced μ_B B apart: at 2 T about 1.16 × 10⁻⁴ eV, 28 GHz, 0.040 nm for the red line at 656 nm","w":["The lowest level is too deep for a field to reach; level 2 splits in two; the magnet gives two lines 0.020 nm apart at 2 T","The average of z is 0 in 1s; level 2 splits by ±3eFr₀; the magnet gives three lines spaced 2μ_B B, 0.080 nm at 2 T","The average of z is 0 in every level, so none moves; the magnet gives three lines spaced μ_B B, 0.020 nm at 2 T"],"why":"0.020 nm at 1 T, and the spacing goes in step with B."},
 {"u":"38·phys.11.1","q":"Why are hydrogen’s corrections from speed of size α² times its energy, which two levels of n = 2 does the count split, and which pair does it leave together?","a":"The energy of motion is mc²(γ − 1), whose series gives a first correction ¾(v/c)² times ½mv², and (v/c)² = 2 × 13.6/511 000 = α²; the count depends on n and j only, so it splits j = ½ from j = 3/2, by about 4.5 × 10⁻⁵ eV or 10.9 GHz, and leaves 2s with the 2p of j = ½, which measurement finds 1058 MHz apart","w":["The corrections are of size α, since v/c = α; the count splits 2s from 2p and leaves the two 2p levels together","(v/c)² = α²; the count splits 2s from the 2p with j = ½ by 1058 MHz and leaves j = ½ and j = 3/2 together","The corrections come from the nucleus moving; the count splits by ℓ only; no pair is left together"],"why":"The 1058 MHz gap is from the electron’s push on the light around it."},
 {"u":"38·phys.12.1","q":"When may the slow-wave technique be used and what does it give inside a wall, and what does the loop-area technique give for the spring, and why is that a check?","a":"Where V changes little over one wavelength; there the wave is about (1/√p) e^(±(i/ħ)∫p dx), and inside a wall, where E is below V, it dies as e^(−∫κ dx) with κ = √(2m(V − E))/ħ; for the spring the loop is an ellipse of area 2πE/ω, so ∮p dx = (n + ½)h gives E = (n + ½)ħω, exactly the levels 35·phys.5 found another way","w":["Anywhere, turning points included; inside a wall the wave stops dead; the spring gets E = nħω","Where V changes little over a wavelength; inside a wall the wave grows; the spring gets E = (n + ½)ħω, which differs from 35·phys.5","Only for flat walls; inside a wall e^(−κL); the spring gets E = n²ħω, as in the box"],"why":"An anexact rule that lands on an exact answer."},
 {"u":"38·phys.13.1","q":"What does the leak-rate technique say, why does it need a band of states, and which earlier technique turns the total of sin²u/u² into π?","a":"A small steady push carries a system from a state i into a band of states, ρ of them per unit energy, at the rate (2π/ħ)|V_fi|²ρ; with one state alone the share swings up and back and never settles, and only a band wide enough to hold the narrowing peak turns the swing into a steady leak; 32e’s transform-energy technique, on a box of width 1","w":["The rate is (2π/ħ)|V_fi|ρ²; a band is needed so that energy is not kept; 22’s geometric sum gives π","The rate is (2π/ħ)|V_fi|²ρ; a band is not needed, one state fills steadily; 32e gives π","The rate is |V_fi|²/ħ; a band is needed to hold more electrons; 21b’s triangle gives π"],"why":"For 2p it gives a life of about 1.6 ns."},
 {"u":"38·phys.14.1","q":"What does the weak-bump technique say, what plays the part of the frequency in the transform, and why does the fading pull give the 1/sin⁴(θ/2) count only as μ goes to 0?","a":"A weak bump turns a wave of push ħk through θ with size f(θ) = −(m/2πħ²)∫V(r) e^(−iq·r) d³r, the bump’s transform; q, the change in the wave’s vector, of length 2k sin(θ/2), stands where the frequency stood; the fading pull g e^(−μr)/r gives f = −2mg/ħ²(μ² + q²), and only with μ = 0, the plain pull between two charges, is |f|² = g²/16E² sin⁴(θ/2)","w":["f is the bump’s height; θ plays the frequency; the count is 1/sin⁴ for every μ","f is the bump’s transform at q; k plays the frequency, of length 2k; the count is 1/sin²(θ/2) as μ goes to 0","f is the bump’s transform at q of length 2k sin(θ/2); the 1/sin⁴ count holds only for large μ, where the pull is short"],"why":"No ħ is left: the waves give the count that orbits alone would give."},
 {"u":"38·phys.15.1","q":"Why does “a field moves every level” fail for hydrogen’s lowest level, and why can the slow-wave technique not be used at a turning point?","a":"To first order the shift is the average of eFz in 1s, and that is 0, the orbital being the same in every direction; and at a turning point p = 0, so 1/√p blows up; the loop-area technique patches it with a quarter wave at each","w":["The lowest level is moved most of all; the slow-wave technique holds best at turning points","The average of z is 0 in 1s; and at a turning point the wave is zero, so nothing is needed","The lowest level has no charge; and at a turning point κ is not defined because V is flat"],"why":"38·phys.10 and 38·phys.12."});
/* end phase F und_r38 */
/* phase F und_r39: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39·phys.1.1","q":"What does the level-spread technique say? Why does a row of N atoms give exactly N levels, and why is the band never wider than 4t?","a":"N atoms in a row, each leaking to its neighbours with energy t, turn one level E₀ into N levels E₀ − 2t cos(kπ/(N + 1)), k = 1, …, N; the N values of k give N different cosines, all the axes of a symmetric map on N places; and each cosine lies strictly between −1 and 1, so every level lies between E₀ − 2t and E₀ + 2t","w":["N atoms keep the one level E₀, filled N times; the band has no width","N atoms give 2N levels E₀ ± kt; the band grows wider without end as N grows","N atoms give N levels E₀ − 2t cos(kπ/(N + 1)); the band is 4t wide only for N = 2"],"why":"N = 6 gives E₀ ± 1.80t, ± 1.25t, ± 0.45t."},
 {"u":"39·phys.2.1","q":"What does the full-band technique say? Why does silicon take in visible light and let longer infrared through?","a":"A band whose every level is filled carries no current, since a push cannot move any electron to a level that is free; silicon’s gap of 1.12 eV is the energy of light of about 1107 nm, so every shorter wave, all visible light, can lift an electron across, and longer infrared has too little energy and passes through","w":["A full band carries the most current; silicon takes in infrared and lets visible light through","A band whose every level is filled carries no current; silicon takes in visible light because its gap is larger than any visible photon","A band carries current only when empty; silicon is clear to all light, since its lower band is full"],"why":"λ = 1240 eV nm / 1.12 eV."},
 {"u":"39·phys.3.1","q":"What does the gap-crossing technique say? Why do germanium switches leak more when warm than silicon ones?","a":"The share of the upper band’s lowest levels that warmth fills is about e^(−E_g/2kT), so a larger gap gives exponentially fewer carriers; germanium’s gap is 0.66 eV against 1.12 eV, and the exponent alone gives it about seven thousand times more carriers per level","w":["The share filled is about E_g/2kT, in proportion to the gap; germanium leaks more because its gap is larger","The share filled is about e^(−E_g/2kT); germanium leaks more because its atoms are heavier","Warmth fills half of the upper band in every material; germanium leaks no more than silicon"],"why":"For silicon at 300 K, e^(−21.7) ≈ 3.9 · 10⁻¹⁰."},
 {"u":"39·phys.4.1","q":"What does the donor technique say? Why does one phosphorus atom in five million matter more than all the warmth of the room?","a":"An extra electron bound by one charge inside a material that shields by ε and makes it move with mass share m* is held like hydrogen’s, with energy −13.6 eV × m*/ε², about −0.026 eV in silicon; that binding is about kT, so warmth frees almost every donor’s electron: 10¹⁶ per cm³, a million times the 10¹⁰ that warmth lifts across the gap","w":["The extra electron is held with hydrogen’s full 13.6 eV; phosphorus matters because it narrows the gap","The extra electron is held with energy −13.6 eV × ε²/m*; warmth cannot free it, so it carries current while bound","The extra electron is held with about 0.026 eV; one atom in five million gives 10¹⁰ per cm³, the same as warmth"],"why":"The binding is a few kT, against 21.7 kT for half the gap."},
 {"u":"39·phys.5.1","q":"What does the junction-voltage technique say? Why is the backward current Iₛ independent of the push?","a":"Where n-type with N_d donors meets p-type with N_a acceptors a barrier of (kT/q) ln(N_a N_d / nᵢ²) forms; a push V forward lowers it, and the current over it grows as e^(V/V_T); the push changes only the height that electrons from the n side must climb, and the flow back over the barrier does not depend on V: it is the −Iₛ of 39d1","w":["A barrier of (kT/q)(N_a + N_d) forms; the backward current grows as e^(V/V_T) too","A barrier forms that a forward push raises; the backward current is 0 at every push","A barrier of (kT/q) ln(N_a N_d / nᵢ²) forms; the backward current is fixed because the supply limits it"],"why":"With N_a = N_d = 10¹⁶ and nᵢ = 10¹⁰ per cm³ the barrier is about 0.71 V."},
 {"u":"39·phys.6.1","q":"Why does a full band carry no current? Why can the barrier not drive a current round a wire?","a":"A push cannot move any electron to a level that is free, since none is; and the barrier is balanced by the spill: joining the ends sets up equal and opposite steps at the contacts","w":["A full band has no electrons to move; and the barrier is too small, under 1 V","A full band carries current only backwards; and the barrier drives a current until the donors run out","The electrons of a full band are held by their atoms; and the wire has no gap for the barrier to push across"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r39 */
/* phase F und_r41: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41·phys.1.1","q":"What is a qubit, and what does the square-chance law say? Why does the state at 30° pass a 45° filter with chance cos² 15°?","a":"A two-way system whose state is a vector ψ = a|0⟩ + b|1⟩ of pairs of complex numbers, of size one, |a|² + |b|² = 1; measured along a right-angled pair d₀, d₁, it gives d₀ with chance |⟨ψ, d₀⟩|² and is afterwards in state d₀; the filter is 15° from the shaking, and |⟨ψ, d₀⟩|² = ((√3 + 1)/(2√2))² = (2 + √3)/4 ≈ 0.933","w":["A bit that is secretly 0 or 1; the law says it gives 0 with chance a; the 45° filter passes it with chance cos 45°","A two-way system with a + b = 1; the law says it gives d₀ with chance ⟨ψ, d₀⟩; the chance is cos² 15° because 45° − 30° is halved","A vector of size one; the law says the state is unchanged by measuring; the chance is ¾ at every filter"],"why":"No single hidden value of 0 or 1 says both ¾ along one pair and 93% along the other."},
 {"u":"41·phys.2.1","q":"What does the no-split technique say, and which fact about products does its proof use? What is E(α, β) for the state Φ, and what does each photon do alone?","a":"The state Φ = (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2 is not a state of the first qubit paired with a state of the second; matching ac, ad, bc, bd to Φ needs ac ≠ 0 and bd ≠ 0 with ad = 0, and a product is 0 only when a factor is (25); E(α, β) = cos 2(α − β); each photon alone passes half the time, whatever the angles","w":["Every state of two qubits is a pair of single states; it uses that products turn about; E(α, β) = cos(α + β); each photon alone always passes","Φ is a pair with a = d = 1; it uses that a product of sizes is a size; E(α, β) = cos²(α − β)/2; each photon alone passes with chance cos² α","Φ is not a pair; it uses that a sum is 0 only when both parts are; E(α, β) = 1 for all angles; each photon alone is stopped"],"why":"Such a state is entangled."},
 {"u":"41·phys.3.1","q":"What does the hidden-answers technique say, and why is S always +2 or −2 for one plan? Which angles make Φ reach 2√2?","a":"For every plan of answers the averages of products obey |E(a, b) + E(a, b′) + E(a′, b) − E(a′, b′)| ≤ 2; S = A(B + B′) + A′(B − B′), and B = B′ gives S = 2AB while B = −B′ gives S = 2A′B, each ±2; a = 0°, a′ = 45°, b = 22.5°, b′ = −22.5° give four terms of 0.707, 2√2 ≈ 2.83","w":["For every plan the sum is at most 4; S is ±2 because A and A′ are equal; the angles 0°, 90°, 45°, 135° give 2√2","For every plan the sum is exactly 2√2; S is ±2 because each product is ±½; any four angles reach it","For every plan the sum is at most 2 in size; S is ±2 because all four answers are +1; the angles 0°, 0°, 45°, 45° give 2√2"],"why":"Measured in 1982 and, with the gaps closed, in 2015: no plan of hidden answers fits."},
 {"u":"41·phys.4.1","q":"What is a density grid, and why do two mixtures with the same grid look the same? What does the half-pair technique say, and why can Seattle not signal to Victoria with it?","a":"ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ ψᵢ*, for a system in state ψᵢ with chance pᵢ; the chance of reading d is ⟨d, ρd⟩, fixed by the grid, so the same grid gives the same chances for every measurement; whatever the Seattle lab measures on its photon of Φ, the Victoria photon alone has the grid ½I, so no choice in Seattle changes any chance in Victoria","w":["A list of the states ψᵢ; two mixtures look the same only when they are the same list; Victoria’s grid depends on Seattle’s angle, so a signal passes","ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ; mixtures with the same grid differ along some filter; the half-pair technique says Victoria’s photon is always level","ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ ψᵢ*; the same grid gives the same chances; Victoria’s grid is ½I only when Seattle does not measure"],"why":"The agreement shows up only when the two labs compare their lists, by an ordinary signal."},
 {"u":"41·phys.5.1","q":"What is a quantum gate, and how do H and the copy gate make Φ from |00⟩? What does the cold-qubit technique say, and why must a 5 GHz qubit be held near 0.02 K?","a":"A grid that keeps every product, run on purpose; H on the first qubit turns |00⟩ into (|00⟩ + |10⟩)/√2, and the copy gate turns |10⟩ into |11⟩, giving Φ; a qubit whose two states differ in energy by hf, left at temperature T, is found in the upper state with chance about e^(−hf/kT) when kT is far below hf; at 300 K, kT is about 1250 hf and the room shakes it at random, while at 0.02 K, hf/kT ≈ 12 and the chance is about six in a million","w":["Any grid of numbers; the copy gate alone makes Φ; a qubit is found upper with chance hf/kT; it is kept cold to save power","A grid that keeps every product; H on both qubits makes Φ; a cold qubit is always found upper; 0.02 K makes hf zero","A gate that forgets a bit; H then copy makes |11⟩; the upper state has chance e^(−kT/hf); a 5 GHz qubit works best at 300 K"],"why":"Some five thousand gates fit before the state is lost to its surroundings."},
 {"u":"41·phys.6.1","q":"What does the period-factor technique say, and why does N divide neither a^(r/2) − 1 nor a^(r/2) + 1? Which step of factoring does the quantum circuit do, and with which transform?","a":"If the period r of a is even and the remainder of a^(r/2) by N is not N − 1, then the gcds of a^(r/2) − 1 and of a^(r/2) + 1 with N are factors of N other than 1 and N; N does not divide the first since r is the least period, nor the second since the remainder of a^(r/2) is not N − 1; the quantum circuit finds the period, applying 32k’s fast transform to a register holding an even mix of every x","w":["If r is odd, a^r − 1 is a factor of N; N divides both factors; the quantum circuit does the gcd, by 4j","If r is even, r/2 is a factor of N; N divides neither because both are below N; the quantum circuit multiplies, by 27j’s elimination","If r is even and a^(r/2) leaves N − 1, the gcds are proper factors; N divides the second factor only; the circuit tries every factor at once"],"why":"For 7 by 15: r = 4, 7² leaves 4, and gcd(3, 15) = 3, gcd(5, 15) = 5."},
 {"u":"41·phys.7.1","q":"Why can the photons of Φ not have carried their answers all along? Why does a quantum computer not simply try every answer at once?","a":"That is a plan of answers, and the hidden-answers technique bounds every plan by 2, while Φ reaches 2.83; and a measurement gives one answer with its chance, so the transform must make the right ones likely","w":["They did carry their answers; the 2.83 comes from signals between the labs; and a quantum computer does read off every answer","A plan of answers reaches 2.83 too; and a quantum computer tries each answer in turn, very fast","The photons have no answers even when measured; and a measurement gives all answers with equal chance"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r41 */
/* phase F und_r42: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·phys.1.1","q":"What is the energy of an arrangement of arrows, and what is its share at temperature T? What does the pair-agreement technique say, and why does it head to 1 as the temperature falls?","a":"E(s) = −J Σ sᵢsⱼ − h Σ sᵢ, the first sum over the links and the second over the dots; its share is e^(−E(s)/kT) divided by Z, the total over every arrangement; two linked arrows with no field agree on average by tanh(J/kT); as T falls, K = J/kT grows, the weights e^(−K) of the disagreeing pairs vanish beside e^K, and tanh K heads to 1","w":["E(s) = J Σ sᵢ; every arrangement has the same share; linked arrows agree by J/kT, which heads to 1 when hot","E(s) = −J Σ sᵢsⱼ; the share is E/Z; linked arrows agree on average by e^(−J/kT), which heads to 1 when cold","E(s) is the number of up arrows; the share is 1/Z; two linked arrows always agree"],"why":"At room temperature with J = 0.01 eV neighbours agree about 69% of the time."},
 {"u":"42·phys.2.1","q":"What does the ring-grid technique say? Why does the average arrow on a long ring head to 0 as the field is taken away, at any temperature above zero?","a":"Z for the ring of N arrows is λ₊ᴺ + λ₋ᴺ, where λ± are the stretches of the 2 × 2 grid T of link factors; for large N the average arrow is m = sinh b/√(sinh² b + e^(−4K)), and as h → 0 the top heads to 0 while e^(−4K) > 0 keeps the bottom away from 0","w":["Z is the sum of the entries of T; the average arrow stays at 1 when the field goes","Z = λ₊ᴺ + λ₋ᴺ; the average arrow heads to 0 only above a temperature of agreement","Z = 2ᴺ; the average arrow is tanh K, which does not depend on the field"],"why":"Walls are cheap on a ring: 2J each, against an entropy gain of about k ln(N²)."},
 {"u":"42·phys.3.1","q":"What does the average-field technique say? Why does m = tanh(τm) have a solution other than 0 only when τ > 1?","a":"If each arrow feels only the average of its q neighbours, then m = tanh((qJm + h)/kT); with no field a solution m ≠ 0 exists exactly when kT < qJ, and just below that m ≈ √3 (1 − T/T_c)^(1/2); tanh has slope 1 at 0 and bends down for x > 0, so the curve tanh(τm) starts above the line m, and meets it again, only when its slope τ at 0 is above 1","w":["m = tanh(qJ/kT) for every field; a solution other than 0 exists at every temperature","m = (qJm + h)/kT; a solution other than 0 exists only when τ < 1","m = tanh(τm) has only the solution 0, since tanh is below 1; agreement never begins"],"why":"For the square grid the guess gives kT_c = 4J; the exact answer of 1944 is about 2.269J."},
 {"u":"42·phys.4.1","q":"How is J for iron read from its measured temperature of agreement? What does the above-the-point technique say, and why does the lean grow without bound as T falls to T_c?","a":"The average-field technique read backwards: J ≈ kT_c/q = 1.381 · 10⁻²³ · 1043/8 ≈ 1.80 · 10⁻²¹ J ≈ 0.011 eV; above its temperature of agreement, in the average-field guess, a magnet’s lean in a small field is h/(k(T − T_c)); the bottom T − T_c heads to 0","w":["J = kT_c · q ≈ 0.7 eV; the lean is h/kT, bounded at T_c","J is read from the pull of two atoms’ magnets; the lean is h · (T − T_c), which heads to 0","J = kT_c ≈ 0.09 eV; the lean is fixed above T_c"],"why":"J is about 190 times the pull of a 1 tesla field on one atom’s magnet."},
 {"u":"42·phys.5.1","q":"What does the downhill-update technique say? Why is the stored pattern ξ a lowest point of the energy when wᵢⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ/N?","a":"Setting one arrow sᵢ to the sign of its field Σⱼ wᵢⱼ sⱼ never raises the energy; with wᵢⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ/N each term −wᵢⱼ sᵢsⱼ is least when sᵢsⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ, which s = ξ gives for every pair at once: E(ξ) = −(N − 1)/2, the least the energy can be, and −ξ is equally low","w":["Setting an arrow against its field lowers the energy; ξ is lowest because its arrows are all up","An update may raise the energy by |fᵢ|; ξ is a highest point, and −ξ the lowest","Updating never changes the energy; ξ is lowest only for N = 4"],"why":"From (+1, +1, +1, −1) one update brings back (+1, −1, +1, −1)."},
 {"u":"42·phys.6.1","q":"Why does agreement between neighbours not give agreement across a long ring? Why is kT_c = 4J not the true value for the square grid?","a":"Agreement between arrows k apart falls as (tanh K)ᵏ, so the ring has none in the whole; and 4J is the average-field guess: the exact value is about 2.269J","w":["Neighbours’ agreement adds up along the ring; and 4J is exact","A ring has no neighbours; and the true value is 8J","Agreement across a ring is tanh K; and 4J is measured on iron"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r42 */
